	<Folder>
		<name>Petrochemical Plants</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>LyondellBasell Wesseling</name>
			<description>Facility: LyondellBasell Wesseling / Knapsack complex
Classification: LyondellBasell&apos;s largest production complex in Europe — integrated steam-cracker and polyolefins site
Location: Wesseling, left bank of the Rhine, adjacent to the Shell Rheinland refineries, south of Cologne, Germany (Knapsack sub-site ~10 miles away in the Chemiepark Knapsack)
Owner/Operator: LyondellBasell Industries (plants operated with Basell Polyolefine); site in operation since 1953, 2.7 km², own 470 MW power plant
Configuration: Two steam crackers (~1.04 Mt/y ethylene combined), four HDPE plants, two LDPE plants, three polypropylene plants and one advanced-polyolefins plant
Main products: Ethylene, propylene, butadiene; polyethylene and polypropylene — total polyolefins capacity above 2.2 Mt/y
Main feedstocks: Naphtha by pipeline from the neighbouring Shell Rheinland refineries and external imports (Shell historically covered about a quarter of the cluster&apos;s cracker feed); trials of Neste bio-based hydrocarbons for partly bio-based PE/PP
Connectivity: Pipeline-linked to Godorf/Wesseling and to Dormagen; olefin product pipelines within the cluster and along the Rhine toward Ludwigshafen, Antwerp and Chemelot; Rhine barge access
Recent data: The end of crude distillation at Shell Wesseling (2025) removed a local naphtha source — the three Godorf/Wesseling crackers need ~3 Mt/y of naphtha; LyondellBasell is building its first commercial-scale MoReTec chemical-recycling plant at Wesseling; the company reviewed its European footprint in 2024 amid ethylene overcapacity
Strategic role: The demand-side half of the Cologne cluster — the reason the naphtha balance of the Rhineland matters; a bellwether for European cracker economics
Risks: Naphtha supply as regional refining shrinks; European ethylene overcapacity and closures elsewhere; Rhine low-water logistics; energy-cost gap versus US and Middle East producers</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>7.005447645998057</longitude>
				<latitude>50.84226741789946</latitude>
				<altitude>0</altitude>
				<heading>0.005919220212794063</heading>
				<tilt>1.79464545559259</tilt>
				<range>11804.49933446533</range>
				<gx:altitudeMode>relativeToSeaFloor</gx:altitudeMode>
			</LookAt>
			<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=000000&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=40</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<gx:drawOrder>1</gx:drawOrder>
				<coordinates>7.002894992897522,50.8164512557096,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>BASF Ludwigshafen</name>
			<description>Facility: BASF Ludwigshafen — the world&apos;s largest integrated chemical complex (Verbund)
Classification: Integrated petrochemical and chemical production site — the anchor of the Rhine&apos;s chemical corridor
Location: Ludwigshafen am Rhein, Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany — left bank of the Rhine opposite Mannheim, ~10 km² site
Owner/Operator: BASF SE (headquarters on site)
Configuration: Two steam crackers plus several hundred interconnected plants — the &quot;Verbund&quot; model in which by-products and heat from one unit feed the next; supplied by naphtha, LPG and natural gas
Main products: Ethylene, propylene and the whole downstream chain — plastics, plasticisers, coatings, agrochemical and pharma intermediates, ammonia and fertilisers
Connectivity: Rhine barge harbour for feedstock intake and product shipment; southern terminus of the Rhine-Main products pipeline (RMR, connected at Oppau); MIDAL natural-gas pipeline endpoint; dense rail network; river water for cooling
Recent data: In August 2026 BASF declared force majeure on DINP and DPHP plasticisers at Ludwigshafen citing extreme weather and severe Rhine logistics constraints; the site turned to shallow-draft vessels; in 2018 and 2022 low water forced output cuts, prompting BASF to charter low-water-capable barges (e.g. Stolt Tankers&apos; special tanker christened at Ludwigshafen in 2023)
Strategic role: The bellwether asset for Rhine low-water crises — the site the market and press watch first; and the largest single consumer node the RMR pipeline was built to serve (with Frankfurt Airport)
Risks: Rhine dependence for both logistics and cooling water; high European gas and energy costs versus US and Middle East competitors; structural downsizing under BASF&apos;s Ludwigshafen restructuring programme; naphtha supply exposure as nearby crude refining declines</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>8.450823352850181</longitude>
				<latitude>49.53131678747764</latitude>
				<altitude>0</altitude>
				<heading>0.02021009179859431</heading>
				<tilt>31.5785025023715</tilt>
				<range>7292.518746099889</range>
				<gx:altitudeMode>relativeToSeaFloor</gx:altitudeMode>
			</LookAt>
			<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=000000&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=4</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<gx:drawOrder>1</gx:drawOrder>
				<coordinates>8.421289375773636,49.52744511654924,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark id="00C02568723EB7B9A5C6">
			<name>MOL Petrochemicals Tiszaújváros</name>
			<description>Classification: Integrated petrochemical complex / cracker and polymers site
Location: Tiszaújváros, Borsod-Abaúj-Zemplén county, northeastern Hungary, on the Tisza river
Owner: MOL Petrochemicals Co. Ltd., part of MOL Group; the synthetic rubber unit is run by ENEOS MOL Synthetic Rubber, a joint venture with Japan&apos;s ENEOS
Capacity: Two steam crackers with about 660,000 tonnes per year of ethylene and roughly 130,000 t/y of butadiene, five polymer units totalling about 765,000 t/y of polyethylene and polypropylene, a 200,000 t/y polyether polyol complex with integrated hydrogen peroxide, propylene oxide and propylene glycol plants, and a synthetic rubber (SSBR) unit; a 100,000 t/y olefins conversion technology unit for polymer-grade propylene is due on stream in 2026 after delays
Main feedstocks: Naphtha and LPG piped from MOL&apos;s Duna refinery at Százhalombatta and Slovnaft in Bratislava, plus imported feedstock; the cracker processed its first ISCC PLUS-certified circular feedstock from post-consumer plastic waste in September 2025
Main products: Ethylene, propylene, butadiene, HDPE, LDPE and polypropylene, polyether polyols, propylene glycol, hydrogen peroxide and solution SBR synthetic rubber
Markets: Hungary and Central and Eastern Europe, with exports to Germany, Italy, Austria and the wider EU converter, automotive and furniture industries
Recent data: On 22 May 2026 an explosion during the restart of the Olefin 1 unit after life-extension maintenance killed one worker and injured nine, four of them severely; MOL said hydrocarbon detonated in a pipeline and found no sign of external interference. The €1.3 billion polyol complex — MOL&apos;s largest ever organic investment, supported by about €131.5 million of Hungarian state aid — was inaugurated in May 2024 and is expected to add close to €150 million a year to group results.
Strategic role: MOL&apos;s principal petrochemical site and the centre of its shift from fuels into chemicals under the SHAPE TOMORROW strategy, which targets processing up to 1.5 million tonnes a year of alternative feedstock by 2030; the polyol complex makes MOL the only company in Central and Eastern Europe covering the full chain from crude refining to polyols. The site employs around 1,100 people and has operated since 1953.
Risks: Process-safety exposure at ageing cracker units, as shown by the 2026 Olefin 1 explosion, weak European polyolefin and polyol margins against imports, high energy and carbon costs, dependence on refinery-supplied naphtha and on Russian-linked crude logistics through the Druzhba pipeline system feeding MOL&apos;s refineries, project delays on the OCT unit, and landlocked inland logistics with no direct seaborne feedstock access.</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>21.0491307468206</longitude>
				<latitude>47.89381560823652</latitude>
				<altitude>91.84558328701513</altitude>
				<heading>0</heading>
				<tilt>0</tilt>
				<gx:fovy>35</gx:fovy>
				<range>6673.615556481527</range>
				<altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode>
			</LookAt>
			<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=000000&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=41</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>21.02832293073626,47.89420784451342,93.98698311388492</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark id="0C008C1AB53EB8584258">
			<name>Dow Terneuzen</name>
			<description>Classification: Mega petrochemical complex / integrated steam-cracker site
Location: Terneuzen, Zeeland, Netherlands — on the Western Scheldt, roughly 30 km north of Antwerp
Owner: Dow Benelux B.V., part of Dow Inc.
Capacity: Approximately 1.8 million tonnes per year of ethylene from three steam crackers (LHC1, LHC2, LHC3), plus about 900,000 t/y propylene, 240,000 t/y butadiene and 900,000 t/y aromatics
Main feedstocks: Naphtha and LPG (propane/butane) in roughly equal shares, delivered by pipeline from Rotterdam and Antwerp as well as by ship and rail, with small volumes of bio-naphtha and pyrolysis oil from recycled plastics
Main products: Ethylene, propylene, butadiene, benzene/toluene/xylene, LLDPE and LDPE, ethylene oxide and glycols, polyols, latex, plus styrene, ethylbenzene and cumene from co-located third-party units (Trinseo, Olin) — around 800 products in total across 16 plants
Markets: Netherlands, Belgium, Germany, France and the wider Northwest European converter and specialty-chemicals industry
Recent data: The 600,000 t/y LHC3 cracker was idled from June 2025 because of weak European demand and restarted in June 2026 as polyethylene and polyurethane chains tightened. In June 2026 Dow announced roughly 605 job cuts in the Netherlands — Terneuzen, Delfzijl and Dordrecht — as part of a global reduction of about 4,500 posts. Terneuzen was spared in Dow&apos;s July 2025 European shutdown package, which instead closed the Böhlen cracker and Schkopau assets in Germany and the Barry siloxanes plant in the UK.
Strategic role: Dow&apos;s largest site outside the United States and its second largest worldwide, with about 3,200–3,500 employees, a 90 MW cogeneration plant, an R&amp;D centre and a service centre covering Europe, the Middle East, Africa and India; it accounts for roughly 45% of Dutch steam-cracking capacity and anchors the Zeeland–Antwerp chemical cluster.
Risks: Structurally weak European cracker margins against low-cost US and Middle East ethane, high gas, power and carbon costs, cheap imports, cracker idling and further restructuring, dependence on delayed hydrogen and CO2 infrastructure (Delta Rhine Corridor, Carbon Connect Delta) for its decarbonisation roadmap, site emissions of about 3.9 Mt CO2/yr, tightening EU environmental rules and Scheldt/Rhine logistics disruption.</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>3.787764908666296</longitude>
				<latitude>51.34081760073316</latitude>
				<altitude>3.924655696200103</altitude>
				<heading>0</heading>
				<tilt>0</tilt>
				<gx:fovy>35</gx:fovy>
				<range>7707.195568123134</range>
				<altitudeMode>absolute</altitudeMode>
			</LookAt>
			<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=000000&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=41</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>3.786962797359981,51.34445943366449,3.911769979405457</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark id="05EE12FD073EB86434EF">
			<name>BASF Antwerp</name>
			<description>Classification: Mega integrated chemical complex / Verbund site
Location: Port of Antwerp, Scheldeland, Belgium — about 6 km² on the right bank of the Scheldt
Owner: BASF Antwerpen N.V., part of BASF SE
Capacity: Around 50 production plants organised into some 15 integrated value chains, anchored by a steam cracker with roughly 1.1 million tonnes per year of ethylene, plus propylene and C4s; ethylene oxide and derivatives capacity was expanded by about 400,000 t/y in 2023
Main feedstocks: Naphtha and LPG sourced globally and delivered by ship, plus natural gas; small volumes of pyrolysis oil from recycled plastics can be co-processed in the cracker
Main products: Ethylene, propylene, butenes, benzene, ammonia, ethylene oxide and glycols, alkoxylates, acrylic acid and superabsorbents, amines, aniline, caprolactam, MDI, polyetherols and surfactants
Markets: Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany, France and the wider European automotive, construction, detergents, packaging and agricultural chemicals sectors
Recent data: In October 2025 BASF Antwerpen raised its structural cost-cutting target from €100 million to €150 million by the end of 2028 and announced roughly 600 job cuts by that date — about one in six positions on the site — including the removal of a third of senior management roles, with no forced redundancies planned and no plant closures announced. In 2025 the Flemish government granted the steam cracker a renewed, indefinite-duration operating permit conditional on selective catalytic reduction on several furnaces and boilers, cutting NOx by about 30% (roughly 220 tonnes a year).
Strategic role: BASF&apos;s second-largest production site worldwide after Ludwigshafen and the largest chemical site in Belgium, employing over 3,000 people; the cracker is described by the company as the beating heart of the site, feeding every downstream value chain, and it forms the core of the Antwerp port chemical cluster with deep-sea, inland waterway, rail and pipeline links to Rotterdam and the Ruhr.
Risks: Weak European chemical demand and margin pressure from low-cost US, Middle East and Chinese capacity — including BASF&apos;s own new Zhanjiang Verbund site — high energy and carbon costs, further cost-cutting and possible additional job losses, tightening Flemish permitting on NOx, nitrogen deposition and PFAS, dependence on still-uncertain CCS and electrification infrastructure for its 2050 climate-neutrality target, and Scheldt/Rhine logistics and port congestion.</description>
