		<Placemark id="0467A13A993EB8752C2C">
			<name>INEOS Antwerp</name>
			<description>Classification: Multi-site petrochemical cluster (existing derivatives plants plus the Project ONE cracker complex under construction)
Location: Port of Antwerp, Belgium — INEOS Oxide at Zwijndrecht on the left bank, INEOS Phenol at Doel, and Project ONE at Lillo/Zandvliet on the right bank
Owner: INEOS Group Holdings (INEOS Oxide, INEOS Phenol, INEOS Olefins Belgium)
Capacity: INEOS Phenol Antwerp runs about 680,000 t/y of phenol with co-product acetone, historically the world&apos;s largest single phenol plant; INEOS Oxide Zwijndrecht produces ethylene oxide and derivatives alongside an ENB unit and its own INESCO combined heat and power plant; Project ONE will add 1,450,000 t/y of ethylene plus a propane dehydrogenation unit
Main feedstocks: Imported US ethane delivered by dedicated gas carriers for Project ONE, propane for the PDH unit, plus benzene, propylene and ethylene supplied by pipeline and the deep-sea ethylene terminal at Antwerp
Main products: Ethylene, propylene, phenol, acetone, ethylene oxide, glycols, glycol ethers, ethanolamines and ethylidene norbornene
Markets: Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany and the wider European polymers, engineering plastics, coatings, surfactants and pharmaceutical intermediates industries
Recent data: Project ONE, a roughly €4 billion investment financed with about €3.5 billion of debt, passed 70% completion in mid-2025 and is designed for CO2 intensity of about 0.29 tonnes per tonne of product — under half that of a typical naphtha cracker. Start-up has slipped repeatedly: the Flemish permit was suspended in 2023 and reissued, and in April 2026 two large cracker modules were stranded in the Persian Gulf by the Strait of Hormuz blockade, missing their scheduled second-quarter delivery. On 10 July 2026 INEOS filed a multi-billion-euro damages claim against the Flemish government over permitting delays.
Strategic role: INEOS is one of the largest employers in Belgian chemicals with around 3,150 staff across 12 sites; Project ONE is the first new steam cracker built in Europe in some 30 years and is positioned as the continent&apos;s lowest-carbon cracker, intended to replace older, less efficient European capacity and anchor INEOS&apos;s olefins position in Northwest Europe. It will create roughly 300 direct permanent jobs plus around 150 contractor roles.
Risks: Further construction and commissioning delays and cost overruns, litigation and permitting uncertainty in Flanders including nitrogen deposition rules, exposure to shipping disruption and freight rates for transatlantic ethane, dependence on the US ethane price advantage and export capacity, weak European ethylene and phenol margins with structural overcapacity, and high electricity and carbon costs for an electrified, power-intensive site.</description>
