		<Placemark>
			<name>RAPID Petrochemical Complex</name>
			<description>Classification: Mega petrochemical complex / integrated cracker and derivatives site
Location: Pengerang, Johor, Malaysia — co-located with the RAPID refinery inside the 2,000-hectare RAPID footprint
Owner: PRefChem (Petronas) for the cracker, with downstream units held by Petronas Chemicals and joint ventures including LyondellBasell, BASF, Evonik, Itochu and PTT Global Chemical
Capacity: A mixed-feed steam cracker complex of roughly 3 million tonnes a year of olefins (ethylene, propylene, C4 and C5), feeding about 4.5 million tonnes a year of petrochemicals; downstream units include 900,000 t/y polypropylene (LyondellBasell technology), LLDPE and HDPE, monoethylene glycol, 220,000 t/y isononanol, 110,000 t/y 1-butene, up to 250,000 t/y hydrogen peroxide with Evonik, plus BASF units for polyisobutylene, non-ionic surfactants and methanesulfonic acid
Main feedstocks: Naphtha and other refinery streams from the RAPID refinery, supplemented by imported LPG and ethane-range feed, plus natural gas and power from the on-site 1.22 GW cogeneration plant and air separation units
Main products: Ethylene, propylene, polyethylene, polypropylene, glycols, isononanol and C4 derivatives, hydrogen peroxide, surfactants and other specialty chemicals; synthetic rubber and elastomer projects have been proposed but not built
Markets: Southeast Asia, China, India and other Asia-Pacific polymer and specialty chemical converters
Recent data: The complex was completed in December 2019 but only reached sustained operation from 2022 after the refinery incidents; cracker output has since tracked refinery availability, with maintenance halts and feed cuts during the 2025 and 2026 gasoline-unit outages. A previously shelved elastomers project has been discussed for revival.
Strategic role: The higher-value half of RAPID, designed to shift Petronas from a fuels producer into specialty chemicals and to capture Asian demand growth for polymers; its integration with the refinery is the main source of the project&apos;s margin uplift.
Risks: Severe regional overcapacity in ethylene and polyolefins driven by Chinese additions, thin or negative cracker margins, complete dependence on the adjacent refinery for feedstock so that any refinery outage cascades downstream, high naphtha feed costs against ethane-advantaged competitors in the Middle East and North America, and delayed or cancelled downstream JV phases.</description>
