		<Placemark>
			<name>Lülsdorf chemical site</name>
			<description>Facility: Lülsdorf chemical site (ICIG, ex-Evonik) — including KFS Biodiesel plant
Classification: Chlor-alkali and speciality chemicals site with an on-site biodiesel producer — the right-bank half of the Wesseling cluster
Location: Niederkassel-Lülsdorf, right bank of the Rhine, opposite Wesseling, south of Cologne, Germany
Owner/Operator: International Chemical Investors Group (ICIG, Luxembourg) since 30 June 2023 — acquired from Evonik together with the associated cyanuric chloride business across the river in Wesseling; more than 600 employees; KFS Biodiesel GmbH operates the biodiesel unit on site
Main products: Cyanuric chloride (crop-protection intermediates), potassium derivatives (food, feed, pharma), sodium/potassium alkoxides — the transesterification catalysts of the biodiesel industry — produced exclusively for Evonik until 2027; biodiesel (FAME)
Connectivity: Rhine barge access on the right bank; integrated with the Wesseling site opposite; road and rail
Recent data: Site divested by Evonik in 2023 as the first step of its Performance Materials exit; ICIG has committed to invest and to no operational redundancies until 2032
Strategic role: A textbook mid-Rhine chemical site — Rhine-dependent for feedstock and shipping, and a small but real node of the European biodiesel supply chain (both catalyst production and FAME output)
Risks: Rhine low-water exposure (2018, 2022, 2026); portfolio-carve-out ownership (financial investor rather than an integrated major); catalyst supply contract with Evonik expiring 2027</description>
