	<Folder>
		<name>Mega-refineries</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Godorf refinery</name>
			<description>Refinery: Shell Rheinland — Godorf refinery (crude processing site)
Classification: Mega-refinery — Germany&apos;s largest refining site and the crude-processing core of the Shell Energy and Chemicals Park Rheinland
Location: Cologne-Godorf, left bank of the Rhine, south of Cologne, Germany
Owner: Shell Deutschland
Capacity: ~187,000 b/d of crude at Godorf; the Rheinland park historically exceeded 17 million tonnes/year (~325,000 b/d) across Godorf and Wesseling combined — Wesseling&apos;s ~147,000 b/d of distillation ended in March 2025, leaving Godorf as the park&apos;s only crude unit
Main feedstocks: Seaborne crude landed at Rotterdam and delivered by the Rotterdam–Rhine Pipeline (RRP)
Main products: Diesel, gasoline, jet fuel, heating oil, naphtha and other petrochemical feedstocks — including the vacuum gasoil now piped next door to feed Wesseling&apos;s base-oil plant
Markets: North Rhine-Westphalia and the Rhineland; naphtha to the neighbouring LyondellBasell and INEOS crackers; products injected into the Rhine-Main pipeline (RMR, whose control centre and pump station sit at Godorf)
Recent data: Since 2025 the park is being reconfigured — crude only at Godorf, base oils and lower-carbon products at Wesseling; on site: a 10 MW electrolyser for zero-carbon hydrogen and a biomethane liquefaction plant; the Godorf Rhine terminal was among the Cologne berths cut off from ARA barges during the 2025 and 2026 low-water episodes
Strategic role: The last crude distillation in the Cologne cluster — the anchor of naphtha supply to one of Europe&apos;s densest petrochemical concentrations, and the origin point of the RMR pipeline
Risks: Cluster-wide naphtha shortfall as regional crude processing shrinks (ICIS estimated a 750,000–1 Mt/y light-naphtha loss from ending Wesseling distillation); Rhine low-water exposure for barge intake/dispatch; European refining margin pressure</description>
