		<Placemark id="ADDPCK00000000000001">
			<name>PCK Schwedt Refinery</name>
			<description><![CDATA[Refinery: PCK Raffinerie Schwedt<br>Classification: Major inland refinery — critical German supply infrastructure<br>Location: Schwedt/Oder, Brandenburg, Germany, near the Polish border<br>Owner: Rosneft ~54% effective (via Rosneft Deutschland and the AET holding — under German federal trusteeship through the Federal Network Agency since September 2022; permanent state control cleared by the European Commission in 2026), Shell ~37.5%, Eni ~8%<br>Capacity: ~11.6 million tonnes per year (~230,000 b/d nameplate); running at reduced utilisation since the loss of Russian pipeline crude<br>Main feedstocks: Historically Russian Urals via the Druzhba pipeline (ended January 2023); since then seaborne crude via the Rostock–Schwedt pipeline and Gdansk, plus Kazakh crude transiting Druzhba — halted by Russia from May 2026<br>Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, heating oil, bitumen<br>Markets: Supplies roughly 90% of fuels for Berlin and Brandenburg, including Berlin airport (BER)<br>Recent data: Russia announced the halt of Kazakh transit deliveries from May 2026; Germany secured a US sanctions exemption for the site; a ~€400 million upgrade of the Rostock supply route has been pursued to raise seaborne capacity<br>Strategic role: The centrepiece of Germany's post-2022 crude rerouting — the refinery that turned the Druzhba cutoff into a live logistics problem<br>Risks: Structural dependence on the small Rostock pipeline; ownership limbo (sanctioned majority shareholder under trusteeship); feedstock diversification costs; utilisation and margin pressure]]></description>
			<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=5d4037&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=40000</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>14.2365,53.0745,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark id="0BAD6318AD3E92734D19">
			<name>Gazprom Neft Omsk Refinery</name>
			<description>Refinery: Gazprom Neft Omsk Refinery
Classification: Mega-refinery
Location: Omsk, southwestern Siberia, Russia
Owner: Gazprom Neft
Capacity: Approximately 22 million tonnes per year, equivalent to roughly 440,000–460,000 barrels per day
Main feedstocks: Russian crude supplied mainly through the domestic pipeline network
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, LPG, bitumen, petroleum coke, sulfur and petrochemical feedstocks
Markets: Primarily Siberia, the Urals and other Russian domestic markets, with selected exports to neighbouring countries
Recent data: In July 2026, a Ukrainian drone attack damaged major crude-distillation units and temporarily halted refinery operations. The site processed about 22 million tonnes of crude in 2024, producing roughly 5 million tonnes of gasoline and 8 million tonnes of diesel.
Strategic role: Russia’s largest refinery by crude-processing capacity and a critical supplier of transport fuels to Siberia and the domestic Russian market
Risks: Long-range drone attacks, unit fires, pipeline disruption, sanctions, equipment-replacement constraints, severe Siberian winter conditions and prolonged maintenance outages</description>
