		<Placemark>
			<name>RAPID Refinery</name>
			<description>Refinery: RAPID Refinery, Pengerang Integrated Complex
Classification: Mega-refinery
Location: Pengerang, Johor, Malaysia — within the Pengerang Integrated Petroleum Complex on the Johor Strait, facing Singapore
Owner: Pengerang Refining and Petrochemical Sdn Bhd (PRefChem). Formerly a 50:50 Petronas–Saudi Aramco joint venture; on 25 May 2026 the partners agreed to transfer Aramco&apos;s stake to Petronas, making PRefChem wholly Petronas-owned
Capacity: 300,000 barrels per day of crude distillation, with atmospheric residue desulphurisation, two gasoline-making units, hydrocrackers and a hydrogen plant; around 9 million tonnes a year of petroleum products
Main feedstocks: Imported crude, historically split between Saudi Aramco term barrels and Petronas supply, landed through the Pengerang deepwater terminal
Main products: Euro 4 and Euro 5 gasoline and diesel, jet fuel, LPG, and naphtha and other streams transferred to the adjacent petrochemical complex
Markets: Malaysia and Southeast Asia, with exports across Asia-Pacific; competes directly with Singapore&apos;s Jurong refining hub
Recent data: One of the two gasoline-making units was down from January to May 2025 for repairs, and a gasoline unit was shut again from February to April 2026, forcing a cut in crude runs. Full commercial operation was only reached in 2022 after a 2019 fire and a 2020 explosion. Aramco&apos;s exit in May 2026 removes its guaranteed crude supply arrangement, though both parties said they would explore continued coordinated crude supply.
Strategic role: Malaysia&apos;s largest refinery and the anchor unit of the country&apos;s flagship downstream project, supplying domestic clean fuels and feeding the integrated petrochemical chain next door.
Risks: Repeated unplanned outages and unit reliability problems, loss of Aramco&apos;s crude offtake certainty, volatile Asian refining margins and new competing capacity in China and the Middle East, freight and feedstock cost exposure on 100% imported crude, and process-safety and environmental risk at a site with a history of fire and explosion.</description>
