	<Folder>
		<name>Refineries</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Wesseling- refinery converting to base oils (2027)</name>
			<description>Refinery: Shell Rheinland — Wesseling site (crude processing ended March 2025; conversion to base oils)
Classification: Former refinery being repurposed — from ~147,000 b/d crude distillation to Germany&apos;s largest Group III base-oil plant
Location: Wesseling, left bank of the Rhine, between Cologne and Bonn, Germany
Owner: Shell Deutschland
Capacity: Historical crude distillation ~147,000 b/d (~7.5–8 Mt/y, about half the Rheinland park); new base-oil unit ~300,000 tonnes/year — roughly 9% of EU and 40% of German base-oil demand
Configuration: The existing hydrocracker is being converted into a Group III base-oil unit; feedstock is vacuum gasoil piped from the Godorf refinery; a 315,000 t/y Shell steam cracker also operates on site
Main products: From 2027, Group III base oils for engine and transmission lubricants; petrochemical feedstocks
Recent data: Final investment decision January 2024; crude distillation ceased March 2025; conversion columns (54 m and 37 m) erected February 2025; start-up planned 2027; expected Scope 1+2 emissions cut ~620,000 t CO₂/year through the end of crude processing and heavy electrification
Strategic role: A live example of European refinery transformation — repurposing rather than closure — and a direct driver of the growing importance of the RMR products pipeline as regional crude runs decline
Risks: European base-oil overhang and price pressure at launch; loss of local naphtha supply for the adjacent LyondellBasell crackers; execution risk on a first-of-its-kind conversion</description>
