		<Placemark id="3DB7582FB24C12451556">
			<name>Mozyr Oil Refinery</name>
			<description>Refinery: Mozyr Oil Refinery
Classification: Large refinery — not a mega-refinery
Location: Mazyr, Gomel Region, Belarus, on the Pripyat River, about 30 km from the Ukrainian border. Coordinates are an approximate placement on the refinery/tank-farm complex on the city’s southeastern edge; not independently verified against a numbered survey source, so worth nudging onto the exact plant footprint once you’re zoomed in.
Owner: Belarusian state, via the Belneftekhim concern, with a substantial minority stake historically held by Russia’s Slavneft (a Gazprom Neft–Rosneft joint venture)
Capacity: Approximately 12 million tonnes per year (roughly 240,000 barrels per day) nameplate; actual throughput has run below capacity since 2022 amid sanctions-related disruption
Main feedstocks: Russian crude delivered via the southern branch of the Druzhba pipeline
Main products: Gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, fuel oil, LPG, bitumen and petrochemical intermediates
Markets: Domestic Belarusian market, with historical exports to Ukraine, the Baltic states, Poland and other European destinations largely curtailed since 2022
Recent data: Under EU, US, UK, Swiss, Canadian and Ukrainian sanctions since 2022; 2026 satellite-imagery reporting noted Russian-supplied air-defense equipment deployed at the site given its proximity to the border; a 2024 storm also forced a temporary outage
Strategic role: One of Belarus’s two major refineries (with Naftan), central to national fuel self-sufficiency and to sustaining Russian crude processing despite Western sanctions
Risks: Sanctions, loss of Western export markets, proximity to the war (~30 km from Ukraine), feedstock dependence on Russia via Druzhba, and equipment/parts access constraints</description>
