		<Placemark id="D5AF86E4D683B6C4439F">
			<name>Yaya Oil Refinery (Yaysky NPZ)</name>
			<description>Refinery: Yaya Oil Refinery (Yaysky NPZ)
Classification: Conventional refinery — not a mega-refinery
Location: Anzhero-Sudzhensk, Kemerovo Oblast (Kuzbass), southwestern Siberia, Russia, in the Sudzhenka railway-station industrial area on the city’s edge. Coordinates are an approximate placement based on the plant’s registered address; not independently verified against a numbered survey source.
Owner: JSC NefteKhimServis, a privately held Russian company (outside the Rosneft, Gazprom Neft and Lukoil groups)
Capacity: 3.3 million tonnes per year primary crude processing (roughly 66,000 barrels per day), plus 1.6 million tonnes/year vacuum distillation and 0.7 million tonnes/year hydrotreating; a third-phase expansion (delayed coking and a depolarization complex, about 98 billion rubles) is under construction, targeting completion in 2027
Main feedstocks: Crude oil and gas condensate delivered by rail
Main products: AI-92/95/98 gasoline, diesel fuel, fuel oil and atmospheric gas oil
Markets: Regional Siberian market (Kuzbass and West Siberia)
Recent data: As of mid-2026 not reported among the roughly ten largest Russian refineries hit by the Ukrainian drone campaign, likely reflecting its distance from Ukraine (over 4,000 km) and smaller scale; Phase 3 construction reported on schedule in 2026
Strategic role: A significant independent (non-major-oil-company) refinery serving the Kuzbass coal and industrial region
Risks: Rail-logistics dependence for feedstock, market and margin exposure as an independent refiner without upstream production, execution risk on the Phase 3 expansion, and exposure to broader sanctions on Russian refined-product exports</description>
