		<Placemark id="84EE0B6A38381F792A47">
			<name>Kulevi Oil Refinery</name>
			<description>Refinery: Kulevi Oil Refinery
Classification: Conventional refinery — not a mega-refinery
Location: Village of Kulevi, Khobi Municipality, Samegrelo-Zemo Svaneti Region, Georgia, Black Sea coast. Sources disagree on the exact distance from the SOCAR-owned Kulevi Oil Terminal (~2 km, ~3 km and ~5 km have all been reported); the plotted point is an approximate placement near the terminal and has not been independently verified against a numbered survey source or dated satellite imagery. Worth confirming visually once zoomed in — look for tall distillation towers, pipe racks and a flare stack, which distinguish it from the terminal’s plain cylindrical storage tanks.
Owner: Black Sea Petroleum LLC; principal beneficial owners reported as Maka Asatiani and Konstantin Gogelia, alongside Dunamis LLC (29.34%) and bank/state financing (Georgian Development Fund, Cartu Bank, Basisbank, Khaliqbank)
Capacity: First phase approximately 1.1 million tonnes per year (about 22,000 barrels per day); a second phase is planned to expand output to roughly 2.9–3.5 million tonnes per year by 2026–2027
Main feedstocks: Crude oil received via the adjacent Kulevi terminal, including — per EU and investigative reporting — Russian-origin crude
Main products: Refined petroleum products including diesel and gasoline
Markets: Georgian domestic market and regional export
Recent data: Construction began 2023–2024 and operations started around 2025; named in the EU’s 21st sanctions package (adopted 23 July 2026) for processing Russian crude, with an unprecedented six-month grace period before restrictions take effect; the operator has said it will stop processing Russian oil by August–September 2026; Transparency International Georgia and OCCRP have alleged the plant may function as a sanctions-evasion channel, citing ownership links to sanctioned Russian oligarch Mikhail Gutseriev and an October 2025 delivery via the sanctioned tanker Kayseri
Strategic role: Georgia’s first and only oil refinery, built as a Black Sea processing hub for Caspian and (per sanctions findings) Russian crude
Risks: EU/Western sanctions exposure, reputational and legal risk from Russian-crude-laundering allegations, ownership opacity, and dependence on the adjacent SOCAR terminal for feedstock</description>
