		<Placemark id="DD4F791C5111D38B2695">
			<name>Kulevi Oil Terminal</name>
			<description>Terminal: Kulevi Oil Terminal
Alternative name: Kulevi Port / Black Sea Petroleum Terminal
Classification: Major crude and refined-products storage, transshipment and export terminal — a combined port and tank-farm facility
Location: Kulevi, Khobi Municipality, Samegrelo-Zemo Svaneti Region, Georgia, Black Sea coast, north of Poti
Owner/Operator: SOCAR (State Oil Company of Azerbaijan Republic), via SOCAR Georgia Petroleum
Main commodities: Crude oil and refined petroleum products, delivered mainly by rail from Azerbaijan and, per EU and OCCRP reporting, Russia; stored and loaded onto tankers for export
Storage capacity: A large multi-tank crude and refined-products tank farm — one of the largest oil storage and loading facilities in Georgia
Connected infrastructure: Rail link to Baku/Azerbaijan, marine loading berths and jetty on the Black Sea, and — since 2024–2025 — the adjacent but separately owned Kulevi Oil Refinery, for which it also handles cargo
Main export markets: Black Sea and Mediterranean export markets via tanker
Recent operations: Continued receiving and transshipping crude — including cargoes linked to Russian origin — through 2025–2026 despite Western sanctions scrutiny; unlike the neighboring refinery, the terminal itself was not named in the EU’s 21st sanctions package as of July 2026; a 2024 Black Sea oil spill was linked to operations in the area
Strategic role: Georgia’s principal Black Sea oil import/export gateway and SOCAR’s main Georgian transshipment hub, now doubling as the feedstock-receiving point for the country’s first refinery
Risks: Sanctions-evasion scrutiny (EU/OCCRP/Transparency International reporting), oil-spill risk, Black Sea shipping-security exposure, and reputational risk from handling Russian-linked crude</description>
