		<Placemark>
			<name>Vadinar Terminal (Nayara Energy)</name>
			<description>Classification: Deep-water crude import &amp; products export terminal — captive port of the Vadinar refinery
Location: Gulf of Kutch, Gujarat, west coast of India
Owner/Operator: Nayara Energy (Rosneft ~49.13%, Kesani Enterprises ~49.13%)
Infrastructure: Single point mooring (SPM) buoys for crude receipt, product jetties, and one of India&apos;s largest tank farms; direct pipeline link to the 400,000 b/d (20 mtpa) Vadinar refinery
Berths / vessel size: VLCC-capable via SPM (up to ~350,000 dwt); products loaded at jetty berths on MR/LR tankers
Strategic role: Main entry gate for discounted Russian Urals crude into India since 2022; major gasoline/diesel export hub toward Africa, the Middle East and Asia; sits ~30 km from Reliance&apos;s Jamnagar complex, making the Gulf of Kutch the world&apos;s densest refining zone (~1.8 million b/d combined)
Recent data: First confirmed Indian fuel shipment TO Russia (Jul 2026) — 356,000 bbl of gasoline loaded at Vadinar on the Agni (Jun 24), transshipped STS off Port Said (Jul 7) onto the Garnet, delivered Vitino, White Sea (Jul 25-26); a rare reversal of the usual India→West products flow
Risks: Sanctions exposure via the Rosneft stake (EU sanctions on Nayara) pushing exports toward shadow-fleet logistics and non-Western buyers; payment/shipping/insurance frictions; cyclone exposure in the Arabian Sea</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>69.87652005038156</longitude>
				<latitude>22.43068468714882</latitude>
				<altitude>0</altitude>
				<heading>0.006173644074275999</heading>
				<tilt>0</tilt>
				<range>60202.05664683969</range>
