				<coordinates>69.7276281862008,22.40079188475431,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Vitino Terminal</name>
			<description>Classification: Small Russian oil terminal on the White Sea — cargo arrives by train, leaves by tanker
Location: Western shore of the Kandalaksha Gulf, White Sea, Murmansk region, Russia (~28 km south of the town of Kandalaksha)
Owner/Operator: Bought with the nearby Belomorsk terminal by the oil trading firm Rusneftekhim in 2009 (~$50 million); restarted recently in the sanctions era
Infrastructure: Connected to the rail network; storage tanks for fuel oil, refined products and gas condensate; one deep-water berth plus two smaller ones — up to three tankers can load at the same time
Berths / vessel size: Can receive tankers up to 230 m long, needing up to 10.9 m of water depth, carrying up to ~80,000 tons of cargo (a mid-size tanker — for comparison, a VLCC carries ~300,000 tons and would be far too big here)
Strategic role: The only oil transshipment port in the White Sea region. In its best years (before 2014) it exported over 4 million tons/year of products from Lukoil and Gazprom Neft, plus Novatek&apos;s gas condensate. Then the flows moved to pipelines and Baltic ports, the port stopped operating entirely and went bankrupt. Today it is coming back to life as a discreet Arctic entry/exit point, far from the closely-watched Baltic routes
Recent data: Under modernization to bring capacity back to at least 4 million tons/year of products (diesel, naphtha). Destination of the first confirmed Indian fuel delivery to Russia: 356,000 barrels of gasoline loaded at Vadinar, transferred to the tanker Garnet near Port Said, delivered here on July 25-26, 2026
Risks: Sanctions logistics (old &quot;shadow fleet&quot; ships, unclear insurance); hard winter navigation in Arctic ice; everything depends on one single rail line; fragile business — the port already died once when the oil flows chose other routes</description>
