			<Point>
				<gx:drawOrder>1</gx:drawOrder>
				<coordinates>7.803447355785695,48.60168919049215,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Birsfelden Tank Farms</name>
			<description>Terminal: Birsfelden port tank farms — Swiss Rhine ports
Classification: Fuel storage within the Swiss Rhine ports complex — the northern half of Switzerland&apos;s barge-fed fuel gateway
Location: Birsfelden, canton of Basel-Landschaft, Switzerland — left bank of the Rhine, immediately upstream of Basel
Owner/Operator: Port area owned by the canton of Basel-Landschaft, managed by Schweizerische Rheinhäfen; tank farms run by private operators — VARO operates two tank farms in Birsfelden (fuel handling and storage); Birsterminal AG runs the largest contiguous cargo-handling facility in the Swiss Rhine ports
Handles: Diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet fuel and other liquid fuels landed by Rhine barge; also general cargo, containers, steel and heavy lift
Energy-transition role: Designated site of the H2-Hub Schweiz — first announced in 2021 by the Rhine ports, VARO, AVIA and IWB, formalised as an association in February 2024 (with GETEC and the Basel Chamber of Commerce); IWB is preparing a hydrogen pilot plant in the Birsfelden port area with green hydrogen production planned from 2026/2027, and imports via the European hydrogen network in the early 2030s; VARO&apos;s stated role is to build a combined hydrogen production and import offering for transport, logistics and industry
Connectivity: End-point of the ARA–Basel barge corridor through the Kaub chokepoint; rail and truck distribution into the Swiss network; supplies the region that also hosts VAROPreem&apos;s Cressier refinery market
Recent data: Together the three Swiss Rhine ports (Kleinhüningen, Birsfelden, Muttenz-Auhafen) handle 6–7 million tonnes a year — about 12% of Swiss imports by volume, and roughly one litre in three of Swiss mineral-oil consumption; a hydrogen hub project on the Birsfelden and Muttenz port areas has been launched with VARO, AVIA and IWB as partners; plans to consolidate tank capacity toward Muttenz-Auhafen and redevelop parts of the Birsfelden site
Strategic role: With Muttenz, the physical entry point of Switzerland&apos;s seaborne fuel supply — where the Rhine corridor from ARA ends and Swiss inland distribution begins
Risks: Total dependence on Rhine navigability (2018, 2022 and record 2026 low water); tank-farm sites constrain port redevelopment; landlocked market with limited alternatives (Marseille–Fos pipeline to Cressier and rail only)</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>7.636677907105755</longitude>
				<latitude>47.55498926526166</latitude>
				<altitude>0</altitude>
				<heading>0.006970041236957261</heading>
				<tilt>3.170333229084024</tilt>
				<range>1840.506306391863</range>
