		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Muttenz Tank Farms</name>
			<description>Terminal: Muttenz-Auhafen tank farms — Swiss Rhine ports
Classification: Switzerland&apos;s main liquid-fuel import and storage terminal — the largest tank-farm concentration in the Swiss Rhine ports
Location: Auhafen, municipality of Muttenz, canton of Basel-Landschaft, Switzerland — first Swiss port reached going upstream, part of the Birsfelden port complex
Owner/Operator: Port managed by Schweizerische Rheinhäfen; principal tank operators TAU Tanklager Auhafen AG (134 tanks, ~530,000 m³ total, up to seven barges discharging simultaneously, rail intake plus 3 rail and 11 truck loading points) and AVIA AG (storage company grouping ten independent Swiss member firms)
Handles: Diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet fuel and other liquid fuels; also edible oils, fertilisers, grains, aluminium oxide and dry bulk
Connectivity: Delivered almost entirely by Rhine barge from the ARA hub via Kaub; onward distribution by rail and truck across Switzerland; designated storage site for fuels held for the Swiss market
Recent data: Built 1937–1940 and repeatedly expanded; ongoing consolidation of Basel-area tank capacity toward Auhafen; part of the H2-Hub Schweiz initiative with VARO, AVIA, IWB and the port authority; directly exposed to the August 2026 record low water at Kaub, when product barges were capped at a fraction of normal loads and ARA–Upper Rhine freight hit ~€200/tonne
Strategic role: The single most important node of Switzerland&apos;s fuel import chain — a landlocked country&apos;s seaborne supply is unloaded here; when barge freight surges, Swiss inland fuel prices decouple from Rotterdam
Risks: Rhine low-water dependence; Seveso-class hazardous-site constraints in a dense urban area; concentration of national fuel imports at one port complex; competition from Fos–Cressier pipeline supply for the Swiss market</description>
