		<Placemark>
			<name>Gustavsburg/Raunheim fuel depots</name>
			<description>Terminal: Gustavsburg &amp; Raunheim fuel depots — Frankfurt Airport supply node
Classification: Refined-products and jet-fuel tank farms at the southern end of the Rhine-Main pipeline — the last storage step before Frankfurt Airport
Location: Ginsheim-Gustavsburg (Main/Rhine confluence, opposite Mainz) and Raunheim (Main), Hesse, Germany — a few kilometres west of Frankfurt Airport
Owner/Operator: Independent tank-storage operators serving the airport&apos;s fuel suppliers; the airport-side kerosene lines are run by dedicated pipeline companies (e.g. Mainline Verwaltungs-GmbH, Dettmer Group, for the second Gustavsburg link opened 2012)
Infrastructure: Tank farms connected to the RMR products pipeline; from here kerosene reaches the airport exclusively by pipeline — two lines from Gustavsburg, one from Raunheim, plus two from the Kelsterbach Main port; the NATO pipeline system also feeds the area
Handles: Jet fuel (kerosene) above all; diesel, gasoline and heating oil for the Rhine-Main region
Connectivity: Southern branch of the RMR pipeline (fork at Udenheim); Kelsterbach river port on the Main for barge-borne supply; short dedicated pipelines to the airport hydrant system
Recent data: Frankfurt Airport receives all its kerosene by pipeline — which is why it stayed supplied through the 2018, 2022 and 2026 Rhine low-water crises while barge-fed regions upstream saw shortages and freight spikes; capacity has been reinforced over time (second Gustavsburg line, 2012)
Strategic role: The point where a national products pipeline turns into airport supply — Germany&apos;s largest hub is fuelled through these tanks
Risks: Concentration of one airport&apos;s fuel supply on a small number of depots and pipes; RMR outage exposure; Main-river low water for the Kelsterbach barge alternative</description>
