	<Folder>
		<name>Ports &amp; Maritime Logistics Hubs</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Duisburg Inland Port </name>
			<description>Hub: Port of Duisburg (duisport) — Europe&apos;s largest inland port
Classification: Inland logistics and industrial port — the pivot of the Rhine barge system between the Lower and Middle Rhine
Location: Duisburg, North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany — confluence of the Rhine and Ruhr, gateway to the Ruhr industrial region
Owner/Operator: Duisburger Hafen AG (duisport), owned by the State of North Rhine-Westphalia and the City of Duisburg; fuel and chemical terminals run by private operators within the port
Main facilities: Extensive container, bulk and liquid terminals; oil and chemical tank storage in the Ruhrort/Hochfeld port areas; steel-industry raw-material handling for the adjacent thyssenkrupp works; trimodal rail/road/water hub
Handles: Refined products and chemicals, coal and iron ore, steel, containers — the full mix of Rhine barge cargo
Connectivity: The point where the deep, wide Lower Rhine meets the shallower Middle Rhine — vessels loaded to ~1,200 t after Duisburg must lighten to ~460 t (and to 300–400 t in August 2026) to pass Kaub; rail links to the Ruhr and to Rotterdam/Antwerp; a Rhine-Main pipeline (RMR) tank farm at Homberg
Recent data: In August 2026 the port reported vessel loading capacities down to about one third of normal, with cargo shifting to smaller vessels, rail and road; the Rhine riverbed partially dried near the thyssenkrupp blast furnaces; Rhine freight for products Karlsruhe–ARA reached a record ~€200/tonne (previous record €130/t in August 2022)
Strategic role: The reference point for Rhine freight economics — &quot;above Duisburg&quot; and &quot;below Duisburg&quot; describe two different barge markets; the last major hub reachable at near-normal loads when Kaub bites
Risks: Low-water exposure downstream of the chokepoint; steel-industry structural decline in its hinterland; competition for scarce rail capacity when barges fail</description>
