		<Placemark id="ADDEPRT000000000001">
			<name>Port of Eilat</name>
			<description>Hub: Port of Eilat
Classification: Israel&apos;s only Red Sea commercial port
Location: Head of the Gulf of Aqaba, Eilat, Israel
Owner/Operator: Privatised in 2013 — operated under concession (Papo Maritime); the EAPC oil jetty sits within the port area
Main activities: Vehicle imports from Asia, potash and mineral exports, general cargo — Israel&apos;s only maritime gateway that avoids both the Mediterranean and the Suez Canal
Recent data: The Houthi campaign against Red Sea shipping from late 2023 collapsed traffic (revenues down on the order of 85–90%), pushing the port into insolvency proceedings in 2024; activity has remained far below pre-war levels since
Strategic role: A live case study of chokepoint economics — the port&apos;s paralysis is the direct cost of Bab el-Mandeb insecurity, visible on one map with the strait itself
Risks: Chokepoint dependence (Bab el-Mandeb); financial fragility; competition from Jordan&apos;s Aqaba next door</description>
