		<Placemark>
			<name>Port Said Anchorage - STS Transfer Zone</name>
			<description>Classification: Offshore ship-to-ship (STS) cargo transfer zone — northern waiting area of the Suez Canal
Location: Mediterranean Sea, off Port Said, Egypt, at the northern entrance of the Suez Canal
Owner/Operator: No single operator — it&apos;s an anchorage (a designated waiting area where ships drop anchor), managed by Egyptian port and canal authorities
Infrastructure: None fixed — that&apos;s the point. Transfers happen ship-to-ship at anchor: two tankers moor side by side with fenders (protective buffers) between them and pump the cargo across with hoses
Why here: Hundreds of ships anchor here every week waiting for their Suez Canal convoy, so two tankers sitting side by side attract no attention; the location sits exactly on the crossroads between Europe, Asia and Africa
Strategic role: One of the main STS hubs of the sanctions era. Tankers loaded with naphtha at Russia&apos;s Baltic port of Ust-Luga carried out STS operations at the Port Said anchorage before delivering to Singapore, and volumes of naphtha and fuel oil transferred at Port Said and Al Hoceima (Morocco) exceeded 200,000 tons in a single month. The logic: scarce ice-class tankers shuttle short trips out of Russian ports and hand the cargo to ordinary tankers in the Mediterranean, which carry it on to Asia — a relay race that saves the specialized ships for where they&apos;re truly needed
Recent data: Transfer point of the Agni → Garnet gasoline cargo (356,000 bbl, Jul 7, 2026) on the first confirmed India → Russia fuel shipment; traders have increasingly turned to STS operations near Port Said as major buyers step back from Russian supplies, with barrels then blended and resold into Asian and African markets
Risks: Cargo origin becomes hard to trace after transfer (that&apos;s the goal for some users, a compliance nightmare for others); aging shadow-fleet tankers with unclear insurance mean any collision or spill could go uncompensated; growing Western scrutiny of exactly these zones</description>
