	<Folder>
		<name>Oil Fields</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Bomu Oil Field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Not operational, per available reporting — shut in since 1993. I&apos;m not fully certain this still holds as of mid-2026: Renaissance announced in July 2026 that it had revived &quot;a previously dormant Nigerian field,&quot; and separately Nigerian officials have discussed resuming Ogoniland production, but no source I found names Bomu specifically as restarted, so I can&apos;t confirm either way with confidence.
Role: Ogoniland field (OML 11), Eastern Niger Delta, Gokana LGA, Rivers State — discovered 1958, shut in since 1993 amid community unrest; historic source field of the Trans Niger Pipeline system; comprises 96 wells, 5 flow stations and the Bomu Manifold, one of Africa&apos;s largest crude/gas gathering points
Operator: Renaissance Africa Energy Company (RAEC, formerly SPDC — acquired March 2025); JV partners NNPC 55%, Renaissance 30%, TotalEnergies 10%, Eni 5%
Capacity: Historic peak of about 75,000 barrels per day (26 oil-producing wells) before the Nigerian Civil War; effectively zero output while shut in
Recent data: Renaissance&apos;s July 2026 announcement (NOG Energy Week, Abuja) of reviving a dormant field, and government statements about Ogoniland (&gt;40% of OML 11&apos;s recoverable reserves) potentially resuming production with 30 Ogoni workers to be hired from January 2026 — neither confirmed as applying to Bomu by name
Risks: Community unrest and sabotage risk tied to the field&apos;s history (1995 execution of the &quot;Ogoni Nine,&quot; including Ken Saro-Wiwa, remains a live grievance); unresolved HYPREP remediation and UNEP-documented soil/groundwater contamination; oil theft and illegal refining common across the wider Niger Delta; reputational risk for any operator restarting without Ogoni community buy-in</description>
			<styleUrl>#scic_oil_field10011</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>7.2835,4.6605,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Nembe Field Area (OML 29)</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — currently producing, though reported output varies noticeably by source (see Capacity/Recent data below), likely reflecting real swings from pipeline vandalism and crude theft rather than a data error on my part.
Role: OML 29 fields (Nembe, Santa Barbara, Okoroba, Oloibiri — Nigeria&apos;s first commercial oil discovery, 1956), southeastern Niger Delta, Bayelsa State; 983 km² block with 11 oil and gas fields; source field for the Nembe Creek Trunk Line (100 km, 600,000 bpd nameplate pipeline capacity)
Operator: Nembe E&amp;P Limited (rebranded from Aiteo Eastern E&amp;P Company, which bought the block from SPDC/Shell in 2015 for $1.7bn), in joint venture with NNPC
Capacity: Reported production figures diverge by source and date — Vanguard (July 2025) cites ~150,000 bopd + 250 MMscf/d gas with a 200,000 bopd target via a new Central Processing Facility and 17 new wells; Guardian (July 2025) cites only ~60,000 bopd with a 100,000–120,000 bopd target, attributing the shortfall to pipeline vandalism and theft that has at times cut volumes by up to 90%. Reserve base is estimated at 2.2 billion barrels of oil equivalent. I can&apos;t reconcile which current figure is more accurate.
Recent data: New Central Processing Facility targeted for Q3 2025; Nigerian officials describe Nembe&apos;s recovery as &quot;critical&quot; to the national 2 million bpd production target (actual national output ~1.4 million bpd as of mid-2025)
Risks: Pipeline vandalism and large-scale crude theft (the dominant risk per recent reporting); major well-blowout risk demonstrated by the November 2021 Santa Barbara Well 1 blowout, which spilled crude for weeks, hit ~40 communities across Bayelsa and Rivers States, and drew criticism over slow federal/regulatory response; community and environmental-compensation pressure; reserve/production volatility tied to security conditions in the wider Niger Delta</description>
			<styleUrl>#scic_oil_field1000</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>6.35,4.53,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Liza Field / FPSO Station</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — Stabroek Block producing 900,000 bopd (Nov 2025) across 4 FPSOs, including both Liza phases
Role: Deepwater field, Stabroek Block, offshore Guyana, ~190 km out, 1,500–1,900 m water depth; developed via two FPSOs, Liza Destiny (Phase 1) and Liza Unity (Phase 2)
Operator: ExxonMobil 45% (operator); Chevron 30% (via Hess acquisition); CNOOC 25%
Capacity: Liza Destiny ~120,000 bopd; Liza Unity up to 220,000 bopd (peak reported at 252,000 bopd)
Recent data: Stabroek Block hit 900,000 bopd in Nov 2025; targeting 1.7 million bopd by 2030 across 8 developments
Risks: Venezuela–Guyana Essequibo/maritime dispute, now before the ICJ; March 2025 Venezuelan naval incursion near the Prosperity FPSO (Payara, not Liza); standard deepwater FPSO operational and spill risk</description>
			<styleUrl>#scic_oil_field100</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>-56.917,8.016999999999999,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Hassi Messaoud Oil Field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — Algeria&apos;s largest producing oil field, still flowing since 1956
Role: Onshore field, Ouargla Province, Sahara Desert, Algeria; anchor field feeding the Saharan Blend export crude
Operator: Sonatrach (state-owned, sole operator)
Capacity: ~350,000 bopd currently; reserves estimated at ~6.4 billion barrels
Recent data: Part of Sonatrach&apos;s 2025–30 investment plan (500 exploratory + 950 development wells nationally, incl. a new 60,000 bopd separation/compression center at Hassi Messaoud); Algeria&apos;s overall reserves (~13 billion bbl) could deplete within ~30 years without new discoveries, per officials
Risks: Natural decline from a mature 1956-vintage field, requiring ongoing enhanced-oil-recovery investment; broader Sahel security concerns near Algeria&apos;s borders (though no attacks on this field specifically found); reliance on continued state investment to offset output decline</description>
			<styleUrl>#scic_oil_field1007</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<coordinates>6.072899999999999,31.6804,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Girassol Field / FPSO</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — producing since 2001, still active as of July 2026
Role: Deepwater FPSO field, Block 17, offshore Angola, ~150 km out in 600–1,400 m water depth; commingles the Girassol, Jasmim and Rosa streams (aka the &quot;GirRI&quot; — Girassol/Rosa/Jasmim Resource Initiative)
Operator: TotalEnergies 38% (operator); partners Equinor 22%, ExxonMobil 19%, Azule Energy 15%, Sonangol 5%
Capacity: Peaked around 200,000+ bopd in the 2000s–2010s, down to roughly 90,000 boe/d by 2021 as the field matures; one of four FPSOs on Block 17 (with Dalia, Pazflor, CLOV) that together produce ~440,000 boe/d
Recent data: Block 17 has produced ~3 billion barrels since 2001, with over 1 billion barrels still estimated recoverable (2019 estimate); no field-specific 2025–26 developments found
Risks: Natural production decline from a maturing 20+ year-old field; standard deepwater operational and spill risk; long-term reserve/output sustained mainly via infill drilling and the wider GirRI redevelopment effort</description>
