		<Placemark id="06E2D3E90A3F2D157379">
			<name>Kirkuk Oil Field (Baba Gurgur)</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational, but far below historical capacity — producing ~350,000–360,000 bopd, with a major redevelopment underway
Role: One of the world&apos;s oldest and largest oil fields, near Kirkuk, northern Iraq; discovered at Baba Gurgur in 1927 by the Turkish Petroleum Company, onstream 1934; disputed territory between Baghdad and the Kurdistan Regional Government; feeds the Kirkuk–Ceyhan pipeline to Turkey
Operator: North Oil Company (NOC, Iraqi state) operates the Baba/Avanah domes plus adjacent Bai Hassan, Jambur and Khabbaz fields (98% of Kirkuk&apos;s reserves); BP signed a finalized contract (March 2025, MoU Jan 2025) to operate a new joint NOC/North Gas Company/BP redevelopment unit
Capacity: Historical peak near 1 million bopd with reserves once estimated over 20 billion barrels; both have fallen sharply from decades of underinvestment — current reserves ~8.9 billion barrels; BP&apos;s initial redevelopment phase targets combined production/reserves of 3+ billion boe (up to 20 billion boe across the wider contract area), aiming to push output toward ~500,000 bopd
Recent data: As of March 2026, Iraq was racing to revive the dormant Kirkuk–Ceyhan pipeline (only ~100 km left to hydrotest, targeting 200,000–250,000 bopd capacity) after southern export routes were disrupted by a Strait of Hormuz closure and Iranian strikes on export infrastructure — national Iraqi output reportedly collapsed from ~4.3 million to ~1.4 million bopd amid that crisis, elevating Kirkuk&apos;s strategic importance
Risks: Nine decades of underinvestment leaving aging, poorly-maintained infrastructure and gas flaring; Kirkuk&apos;s disputed Baghdad–KRG status; broader 2026 regional conflict risk (Hormuz closure, Iranian strikes) disrupting Iraq&apos;s main southern export routes and elevating reliance on this more vulnerable northern corridor; execution risk on the new BP-operated redevelopment</description>
