		<Placemark id="072AC446C03F2D28A0C3">
			<name>Taq Taq oil field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Not operating — production ceased before the 2023 KRG export-pipeline closure, and Genel produced nothing from the field in 2024; Genel formally agreed exit terms with the KRG in January 2025, so as of the most recent reporting the field is shut in with no confirmed operator or restart plan
Role: Onshore field near the town of Taq Taq, Kurdistan Region, Iraq; PSC first signed by Genel Energy in 2002; was once the largest internationally-operated field in Kurdistan
Operator: Genel Energy (operator, historically ~44% — exiting); partners historically included Sinopec/Addax Petroleum (~36%) and the Kurdistan Regional Government (~20%); I couldn&apos;t independently re-confirm these exact percentages as still current, given the exit is underway
Capacity: Peaked at 116,000 bopd in 2015; fell sharply after 2017 due to geological complications and reserve downgrades, down to under 5,000 bopd before the 2023 pipeline closure, then to zero
Recent data: Genel exited the Taq Taq PSC (terms agreed Jan 2025) to shed residual decommissioning liability, leaving its Kurdistan presence reduced to a 25% non-operated stake in DNO&apos;s Tawke field; separately, the wider KRG–Turkey export pipeline (Kirkuk–Ceyhan) reopened in September 2025 after a 2.5-year halt, with the export deal since extended to March 2026 — a development that could theoretically support a Taq Taq restart under a new operator, though none has been confirmed
Risks: Field abandonment/decommissioning liability now shifting to remaining/incoming partners; steep, poorly-understood geological decline that ended Genel&apos;s operatorship; Kurdistan-Baghdad political and pipeline-access risk; no confirmed path back to production</description>
