		<Placemark id="0B5E62717C3F3CCE416C">
			<name>Ahdab Oil Field ( Al-Waha Petroleum Company)</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — producing since 2011, with output ramping up sharply in 2025
Role: Onshore field, Wasit Governorate, Iraq (32.42°N, 45.71°E); discovered 1979, developed under one of Iraq&apos;s earliest post-2008 technical service contracts
Operator: China National Petroleum Corporation (CNPC), via its Iraq subsidiary Al-Waha Petroleum Company; partners Midland Oil Company (MdOC, Iraqi state) and China ZhenHua Oil
Capacity: ~43.8 million bbl/year (~120,000 bopd) in 2025, up sharply from ~16.8 million bbl/year (~46,000 bopd) in 2023; ~1 billion barrels of oil reserves plus substantial associated gas (~21.2 bcm reserves, ~5 bcm cumulative gas production by 2024)
Recent data: April 2025 — MdOC and Al-Waha restarted a previously idle vertical well (ADR3-3ST) using horizontal drilling, yielding strong output with low water cut; framed as a repeatable model for reviving other idle wells across the field
Risks: Reliance on aging/idle wells requiring workover investment to sustain the recent production growth; Iraqi political, security and contractual (service-fee vs. profit-sharing) risk typical of CNPC&apos;s Iraq portfolio; water-handling/reservoir-management challenges in older wells</description>
			<LookAt>
