		<Placemark id="054A8A275C3F3CCF9266">
			<name>East Baghdad Field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Not yet clearly confirmed as producing — as of the most recent reporting, first oil was targeted for June 2026 and first gas for September 2026 (drilling of 18 planned wells was underway); I could not confirm whether that startup has actually occurred as of today
Role: Iraqi &quot;super-giant&quot; oil (and gas) field literally underlying populated parts of eastern Baghdad, 11 km wide x 64 km long; historically stranded because of its urban location and, for years, a lack of international bidder interest (JAPEX talks fell through in 2009)
Operator: Midland Oil Company (MdOC, Iraqi state), developing jointly with EBS Petroleum (representing China&apos;s ZhenHua Oil); Iraqi Drilling Company (IDC) handling well drilling
Capacity: Original 2009-era estimate was 8 billion barrels recoverable / up to 400,000 bopd potential; reserves have since been revised up to 15 billion barrels (from 13bn) following new discoveries, making it central Iraq&apos;s largest oil resource; near-term target is 120,000 bopd oil plus 25–50 MMcf/d gas
Recent data: Contract area expanded by 95 km² west of the Diyala River in February 2025, adding ~710 million barrels of estimated resources; part of Iraq&apos;s broader 2026 wave of major oil-project startups
Risks: Urban/populated-area development constraints (drilling and infrastructure under inhabited Baghdad neighborhoods); Iraqi political and security risk; execution risk on an aggressive 2026 startup timeline; historical precedent of stalled development due to lack of investor interest</description>
			<LookAt>
