		<Placemark>
			<name>Hebron-Ben Nevis oil field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational, though it experienced production curtailments in 2025 — a January 2025 shuttle-tanker collision at the Whiffen Head terminal jetty plus a shuttle-tanker shortage and bad weather cut output for weeks; Wikipedia also notes the platform &quot;under maintenance since 2025,&quot; which I couldn&apos;t independently confirm as an extended shutdown versus normal turnaround maintenance
Role: Gravity-based structure (GBS) platform, Jeanne d&apos;Arc Basin, offshore Newfoundland, ~350 km SE of St. John&apos;s, 92 m water depth; Canada&apos;s largest offshore oil platform
Operator: ExxonMobil Canada 36% (operator); Chevron 27%; Suncor Energy 23%; Equinor 10%; Newfoundland and Labrador Hydro 4%
Capacity: Platform designed for ~150,000 bopd average over a 30-year life, with peak capacity around 250,000 bopd; ~700 million barrels of producible hydrocarbons in place
Recent data: Discovered 1980, onstream 2017; a December 2023 regulatory approval added previously excluded Jeanne d&apos;Arc formation sands with reserves revised sharply upward (Jeanne d&apos;Arc formation 132→165 MMboe; Pool 1 560→596 MMbbl; Hibernia formation 15→59 MMbbl), using existing infrastructure with no new drill centers
Risks: Harsh North Atlantic weather affecting shuttle-tanker logistics and export; shared exposure to shuttle-tanker/terminal incidents (as in the Jan 2025 Whiffen Head collision); standard GBS/deepwater operational risk; multi-partner JV coordination</description>
