		<Placemark>
			<name>Cold Lake Blend Production Facility</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — continuously producing since 1985 (pilot since 1975), currently ramping up via new SAGD conversion projects
Role: In-situ oil sands complex, Cold Lake region, Alberta, Canada, extracting bitumen from the Clearwater formation across multiple pad areas (Kirby, Mahihkan, Leming, Maskwa, Mahkeses, Nabiye); produces the &quot;Cold Lake Blend&quot; (CL) heavy crude, moved via the Cold Lake Pipeline to Edmonton/Hardisty
Operator: Imperial Oil, 100% owned and operated
Capacity: ~190,000 bbl/d design capacity; current output ~150,000 bbl/d, targeted to reach 165,000 bbl/d by 2030
Recent data: Transitioning from legacy Cyclic Steam Stimulation (CSS) to Solvent-Assisted SAGD — Grand Rapids SA-SAGD onstream since 2024; Leming SAGD began steam injection summer 2025 with first oil expected end-2025; Mahihkan SA-SAGD targeted for 2029; combined projects expected to add ~50,000 bbl/d by 2030 while cutting the steam-to-oil ratio from 4.4 to a targeted 3.5
Risks: Natural-gas price exposure (steam generation is gas-intensive); Cold Lake Blend/heavy-oil price discount to WTI; execution risk converting decades-old CSS infrastructure to SA-SAGD; Canadian oil-sands emissions-cap and carbon-pricing regulatory exposure; pipeline egress/takeaway capacity dependence</description>
