		<Placemark id="0F0D10EA6840677FCB7A">
			<name>Ras Shukier oil field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — active field and Egypt&apos;s key Gulf of Suez export hub
Role: Field and major crude-export terminal, Ras Shukheir, Red Sea Governorate, ~north of Hurghada, Gulf of Suez, Egypt; terminal aggregates and loads crude from multiple GUPCO-area fields (incl. Morgan, discovered 1967); feeds the 340 km Ras Shukeir–Asyut pipeline (operator: EGPC&apos;s Petroleum Pipeline Company) to inland refineries
Operator: GUPCO (Gulf of Suez Petroleum Company), a 50/50 BP–EGPC joint venture; a separate, smaller adjacent concession (the 40 km² Offshore Shukheir Marine Concession) is run by OSOCO, currently farmed out to PETZED Investments
Capacity: Field/terminal-specific bpd figures weren&apos;t available in current sourcing; GUPCO&apos;s wider Gulf of Suez operations were producing ~71,250 bopd as of a 2026 report (highest since July 2019), with incremental additions from fields like North Safa
Recent data: GUPCO&apos;s Gulf of Suez concession was extended in 2005 for 20 more years (through ~2025), alongside $1.4bn of past infrastructure/exploration investment; I could not confirm a Ras Shukheir-specific 2025–26 update beyond this general GUPCO-area activity
Risks: Mature, decades-old Gulf of Suez basin with natural decline risk; concession-renewal/political risk tied to EGPC terms; fragmented multi-operator concession structure (GUPCO vs. OSOCO) around the same area adds some ownership complexity; standard marine-terminal and pipeline spill/operational risk</description>
