		<Placemark id="0B88D3B248406780BADA">
			<name>Zeit Bay oil field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — mature, declining field still producing as of the mid-2010s onward (no evidence of shut-in)
Role: Onshore/coastal field, Gulf of Suez, Egypt, ~80 km north of Hurghada; one of three core concessions (with Ras Fanar and Ras Budran) run by Suez Oil Company (SUCO); has an associated Zeit Bay export terminal; notable for producing from a fractured basement reservoir
Operator: Suez Oil Company (SUCO), a 1979 JV between EGPC (Egypt&apos;s state oil company) and the German DEA stake — now held by Harbour Energy following its 2024 acquisition of Wintershall Dea&apos;s E&amp;P business
Capacity: Reserves ~215 million boe (1996 estimate); by the mid-2010s roughly 80% of existing-well resources were already extracted; Zeit Bay-specific output was small (~4,850–5,500 bopd) from 19 wells, sustained via artificial lift and secondary recovery
Recent data: Ownership transferred from Wintershall Dea to Harbour Energy in 2024 as part of an $11.2bn global asset-portfolio deal; no Zeit Bay-specific 2025–26 news found — broader GUPCO-area Gulf of Suez fields (a separate JV) have seen incremental new-well additions in 2026
Risks: Deep field maturity/depletion (~80%+ of accessible reserves already produced), requiring continued secondary-recovery investment just to hold output; relatively high lifting costs (~$9.60/bbl) typical of a declining mature basin; ownership-transition integration risk following the Harbour Energy takeover</description>
