		<Placemark id="0501746E9A402134DD91">
			<name>Forties field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Still operational, but in active wind-down — very low output (~10,000 bopd as of 2025) and decommissioning contracts already being let for some wells, even though Apache disputes reports of an imminent 2026 full shutdown
Role: Historic North Sea field, UK sector, 110 miles ENE of Aberdeen, 350 ft water depth; first oil discovered by BP in 1970, onstream 1975; feeds the Forties Pipeline System (FPS), sold by BP to INEOS in 2017
Operator: Apache/APA Corporation (bought BP&apos;s 96% stake in 2003 for $812m); pipeline system separately owned by INEOS FPS
Capacity: Peaked at 500,000 bopd in 1979; fell to ~41,700 bopd (2013), ~20,000 bopd (2021), and ~10,000 bopd (2025); oil-in-place was 4.2 billion barrels at the 2003 BP sale, plus ~800 million barrels found since by Apache
Recent data: A 2025 court dispute over a Decommissioning Security Agreement (with BP, Shell, Esso/ExxonMobil, NEO Energy) modeled a 2026 cessation date; Apache disputes this as a mischaracterization and is seeking to push the modeled cessation to 2030+, while cutting capex sharply and halting new North Sea drilling; a multi-year plug-and-abandonment campaign with Well-Safe Solutions began in 2026
Risks: Imminent end-of-field-life/decommissioning risk regardless of exact COP date; UK windfall tax cited by Apache as a deterrent to further investment; legal/financial dispute risk among JV partners over decommissioning liability; 50-year-old infrastructure integrity risk</description>
