		<Placemark id="041F6D2F9A3F2D8A9E60">
			<name>Azeri–Chirag–Gunashli (ACG) offshore fields</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — producing since November 1997, now also entering gas production as of 2026
Role: Giant offshore oil (and now gas) complex, Caspian Sea, ~120 km off Azerbaijan&apos;s coast (40°01&apos;N, 51°16&apos;E); comprises the Azeri, Chirag and Deepwater Gunashli fields across a 432 km² development area; oil evacuated via existing Sangachal Terminal infrastructure
Operator: BP 30.37% (operator, as AIOC consortium); SOCAR 31.66%; MOL 9.57%; INPEX 9.31%; ExxonMobil 6.79%; TPAO 5.73%; Itochu 3.65%; ONGC Videsh 2.93% (Q1 2024 shares)
Capacity: Peaked at 885,000 bopd in 2010; down to ~339,000 bopd by Q1 2024 as the field matures; oil in place ~5 billion bbl with 5–6 billion bbl recoverable; newly confirmed non-associated gas resource of ~4 Tcf (up to 6 Tcf potential)
Recent data: PSA extended in 2017 to run until 2049; BP began first commercial non-associated gas production in 2026 (from West Chirag, Qirmaki Upper/Lower Sand reservoirs), repositioning ACG as an integrated oil-and-gas asset partly to support gas supply to Europe
Risks: Long-term oil-output decline from a mature (peaked 2010) field; execution and reservoir risk on the new gas development; geopolitical exposure tied to Caspian/Azerbaijan energy relations with Europe; concentration risk given BP&apos;s operatorship and multinational partner structure</description>
