		<Placemark>
			<name>Ekofisk oil field</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — producing continuously since June 1971, with license life extended to 2048
Role: Norway&apos;s first major North Sea oil discovery (1969) and hub of the &quot;Greater Ekofisk Area&quot;; Norwegian sector, ~70 m water depth; chalk reservoir field famous for seabed subsidence and a platform-jacking fix
Operator: ConocoPhillips Skandinavia AS 35.11% (operator); TotalEnergies 39.90% (largest stakeholder); Vår Energi 12.39%; Equinor 7.60%; Petoro (Norwegian state) 5%
Capacity: Original recovery expected at only 17% via pressure depletion; sustained water injection has lifted expected final recovery to over 50%; total investment through 2024 was NOK 167.6 billion, with NOK 13.5 billion more planned from 2025
Recent data: Production licenses extended through 2048 (from a 2028 expiry), enabling up to ~80 years of output; a $1.8bn &quot;PPF&quot; project sanctioned Dec 2025 will revive three shut-in gas-condensate fields (Albuskjell, Vest Ekofisk, Tommeliten Gamma) tied back to the Ekofisk Complex, targeting 90–120 MMboe and first production Q4 2028; Eldfisk North development plan also in progress
Risks: Chalk-reservoir compaction/subsidence — seabed sank several meters in the 1980s, requiring a record-setting 1987 jack-up of five platforms by ~6 m; historical major-incident precedent (1977 Bravo blowout, the North Sea&apos;s largest, ~80,000–126,000 bbl spilled); reliance on continuous water injection and infill drilling to sustain output from a 50+ year-old field</description>
