		<Placemark>
			<name>Johan Sverdrup</name>
			<description><![CDATA[Operational status: Operational — producing since October 2019, though now entering natural decline
Role: Giant offshore field, Utsira High, Norwegian North Sea; Norway's largest producing oil field and the North Sea's 3rd largest ever; supplies power to neighboring Edvard Grieg, Gina Krog and Ivar Aasen fields
Operator: Equinor 42.63% (operator); Aker BP 31.57%; Petoro (Norwegian state) 17.36%; TotalEnergies 8.44%
Capacity: Plateau capacity of 755,000 boe/d — up to 30% of total Norwegian Continental Shelf oil output; ~2.7 billion boe recoverable resources, targeting a 70%+ recovery factor; exceptionally low carbon intensity (0.67 kg CO2/barrel vs. ~15 kg global average, via shore-power electrification)
Recent data: Equinor guided (Feb 2026) to output declining >10% but <20% in 2026 as the field matures; Phase 3 sanctioned July 2025 ($1.3bn, 8+ new wells, 40–50 MMboe, startup 2027) to slow the decline; Phase 4 now being pursued around the new Tonjer/Geitungen discoveries (20–30 MMboe), targeting 2029 startup as a subsea tieback
Risks: Natural field decline now underway despite mitigation drilling; execution risk on sequential Phase 3/Phase 4 tie-backs; North Sea mature-basin cost inflation; otherwise low ESG/regulatory risk given the field's low-emissions profile]]></description>
