		<Placemark>
			<name>WTI midland basin oil fields</name>
			<description>Operational status: Operational — actively drilled, high-volume producing basin as of 2026
Role: Sub-basin of the Permian Basin, West Texas (Midland, Ector, Martin, Howard, Reagan, Upton counties etc.); source of the WTI Midland crude grade, now the world&apos;s largest freely traded oil grade by volume and a Platts Dated Brent benchmark component since May 2023
Operator: Fragmented among many producers; dominant Midland-Basin footprints held by ExxonMobil (via its 2024 Pioneer Natural Resources acquisition) and Diamondback Energy
Capacity: Texas-side Permian output (Midland-dominated) ran ~5.8 million bopd in February 2026 vs. ~2.3 million bopd on the New Mexico/Delaware side; Diamondback alone produced 521 Mboe/d in Q1 2026 at the basin&apos;s lowest operating costs
Recent data: Midland described as &quot;the more mature acreage&quot; with a finite Tier 1 drilling inventory, while growth is increasingly shifting to the Delaware Basin; large-scale 2024–25 consolidation (Exxon-Pioneer, Diamondback-Endeavor) has concentrated Midland output among fewer, larger operators
Risks: Tier 1 drilling-inventory exhaustion limiting future growth; WTI Midland–Brent/Cushing differential volatility affecting drilling economics; produced-water disposal and induced seismicity in West Texas; associated-gas takeaway/flaring constraints; exposure to OPEC+ supply decisions and global price swings</description>
