	<Folder>
		<name>Pipelines crude</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Fishkhabur-Ceyhan pipeline (Turkish leg)</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — international transit; Iraq&apos;s only large export route that never touches the Strait of Hormuz

Route: Fishkhabur border crossing → Silopi → Cizre → Mardin → Şanlıurfa → Adıyaman → Kahramanmaraş → Ceyhan tank farm and Yumurtalık marine terminal; ~625 km through five pumping stations

Owner/Operator: BOTAŞ, the Turkish state pipeline company

Capacity: Nameplate up to 1.6 million b/d; currently running at roughly 15% of that, constrained entirely by upstream supply rather than the pipe

Operating history: In service since 1976; shut March 2023 when the ICC ordered Turkey to pay Iraq ~$1.5 billion for accepting unauthorised KRG cargoes — the only case of a transit state closing a major line by court order rather than by conflict. Restarted September 2025, halted, resumed March 2026

Recent data: Turkey terminated the 1973 agreement on 27 July 2026 with a twelve-month wind-down while a replacement energy framework is negotiated; flows were expected to continue uninterrupted through the expiry

Strategic role: The Hormuz hedge. With southern Iraqi exports exposed to Gulf closure, this is the country&apos;s only Mediterranean outlet — and it shares Ceyhan with BTC, stacking Iraqi and Caspian contingency plans on a single coastline

Risks: Treaty vacuum after July 2026 and tariff renegotiation from a position of Turkish strength; security across southeastern Anatolia; seismic exposure — the 2023 Kahramanmaraş earthquake suspended Ceyhan loadings; and terminal concentration, where one damaged outlet removes two countries&apos; backup routes simultaneously</description>
			<styleUrl>#msn_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
