		<Placemark id="ADDEAPC000000000001">
			<name>Eilat–Ashkelon Pipeline (EAPC, Trans-Israel)</name>
			<description><![CDATA[Classification: Crude oil pipeline — reversible Red Sea ↔ Mediterranean land bridge (Trans-Israel / EAPC line)<br>Route: Eilat oil port (Gulf of Aqaba) → Arava valley → Negev → Ashkelon oil port (Mediterranean); 254 km, 42-inch — the backbone of a ~747 km EAPC network that also feeds the Haifa and Ashdod refineries<br>Owner/Operator: Europe Asia Pipeline Company (EAPC) — Israeli state-owned; operations historically under strict confidentiality dating from the company's origins<br>Capacity: ~30 million tonnes/year of crude; cited at ~400,000 b/d northbound and up to ~1.2 million b/d in reverse flow (reverse project completed 2003)<br>Operating history: Built 1968 as a covert 50/50 Israeli–Iranian venture (via Liechtenstein shell companies) to move Iranian crude to Europe bypassing Suez; the partnership collapsed with the 1979 revolution and international arbitration later ruled in Iran's favour for over a billion dollars — never paid; the 49-year concession expired in 2017 and activities passed to the renamed Europe Asia Pipeline Company<br>Recent data: The 2020 MED-RED Land Bridge MOU with UAE interests (post-Abraham Accords) was frozen by Israel's Environment Ministry — a 'Zero Additional Risk' policy for the Gulf of Eilat (Nov 2021) blocks any increase in tanker calls; a January 2025 State Comptroller report flagged the company's challenges at Eilat; EAPC is diversifying, including a 260 km fibre-optic corridor along the pipeline<br>Strategic role: The only Suez-bypass land bridge under a single state's control, connecting Red Sea and Mediterranean tanker markets in both directions<br>Risks: War exposure at both ends (rocket range of Gaza at Ashkelon, Red Sea shipping crisis at Eilat); environmental veto on expansion — the 2014 Arava spill from this line remains one of Israel's worst ecological disasters; opacity of actual flows]]></description>
			<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
