		<Placemark id="ADDBPS00000000000001">
			<name>Baltic Pipeline System (BPS-1)</name>
			<description><![CDATA[Classification: Crude oil pipeline system — Russia's Baltic export artery (BPS-1)<br>Route: Timan-Pechora / West Siberia / Urals-Volga networks → Yaroslavl → Kirishi (KINEF refinery) → Primorsk oil terminal, Gulf of Finland<br>Owner/Operator: Transneft<br>Capacity: Up to ~75 million tonnes per year (~1.5 million b/d system capacity); Primorsk loads ~1 million b/d of Urals crude plus ~300,000 b/d of diesel; commissioned December 2001<br>Crude: Urals — Russia's flagship export grade, since 2022 shipped largely via the sanctions-era 'shadow fleet' under the G7 price-cap regime<br>Status: Operational but repeatedly disrupted — Ukrainian long-range drones forced the first loading suspension at Primorsk in September 2025, with renewed strikes in March and May 2026 halting exports again; at the peak, roughly 40% of Russia's oil export capacity was assessed offline<br>Strategic role: Built to bypass the Baltic transit states and concentrate exports at a Russian-controlled port; the single most important outlet for seaborne Urals<br>Risks: Primary target of the Ukrainian deep-strike campaign on energy infrastructure; sanctions and price-cap enforcement; winter ice navigation in the Gulf of Finland; single-corridor concentration]]></description>
