		<Placemark id="0A981C3A6A3F2D3C6D14">
			<name>Kirkuk-Fishkhabur Pipeline (federal Iraqi leg)</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Baghdad&apos;s sovereign northern outlet; dormant since 2014 and currently the single most consequential reconstruction project in Iraqi midstream

Route: Kirkuk K-1 station → Baiji junction → northwest along the Tigris to Fishkhabur on the Turkish border; ~345 km, twin 46&quot;/40&quot; lines commissioned 1976 and 1987. The rebuild reroutes through Salahaddin and Nineveh, deliberately avoiding Kurdistan Region territory

Owner/Operator: Iraqi Oil Ministry via North Oil Company; forms the Iraqi half of the ITP under the 1973 Iraq–Turkey agreement

Capacity: Historic ITP design up to 1.6 million b/d; the rebuilt Kirkuk–Nineveh segment is specified at 350,000 b/d

Operating history: Carried Kirkuk crude from 1976; degraded through sanctions and repeated insurgent bombings after 2003; destroyed during the 2014 ISIS advance and inactive for twelve years. Federal Kirkuk barrels have moved through the Kurdistan Region&apos;s system since 2015

Recent data: Hydrostatic testing on the 48-inch Baiji–Fishkhabur line was underway in March 2026; the Oil Ministry targeted June 2026 for the Kirkuk–Nineveh segment while conceding final repairs would take several more months. No confirmation it is carrying export barrels — federal crude still routes via the Saralo pumping station inside the KRI

Strategic role: Sovereignty restoration. Completion ends Baghdad&apos;s physical dependence on a pipe it does not own, converting a hostage situation into an ordinary budget dispute

Risks: Chronic schedule slippage; security across Nineveh and Salahaddin; funding and Chinese contractor dependence; Kirkuk upstream deliverability after two decades of underinvestment; and a political risk on completion — stranding KEP capacity reopens the Erbil fight on new terms</description>
			<LookAt>
