		<Placemark>
			<name>East–West Crude Oil Pipeline - Petroline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Gulf → Red Sea trans-Arabian land bridge; the largest Hormuz bypass in existence and the backbone of Saudi export resilience

Route: Abqaiq processing complex (Eastern Province) → ~1,200 km westward across the peninsula through a chain of pumping stations → Yanbu terminals and refining hub on the Red Sea; runs as parallel lines (48-inch and 56-inch), with the IPSA line built for Iraq tracking part of the corridor

Owner/Operator: Saudi Aramco — wholly state-controlled, operated as an integral part of the kingdom&apos;s export system

Capacity: ~5 million b/d nameplate; one of the two parallel lines spent years converted to gas service before being returned to crude to restore full capacity, and reported plans would expand the system by a further 2 million b/d; normal peacetime utilization was modest — the line existed primarily as insurance

Operating history: Built in the early 1980s during the Iran–Iraq &quot;Tanker War&quot; precisely to hedge Hormuz risk; the insurance was tested before — Houthi drones struck two of its pumping stations in May 2019, and the Abqaiq processing plant at its inlet was hit that September — but 2026 is the first time the line has become the kingdom&apos;s primary export artery rather than its backup

Recent data: Since the March 2026 Hormuz closure, Aramco pivoted loadings to Yanbu, keeping Saudi Arabia at 57% of its 2025 export rate in May — versus 82% for the UAE and 8% for Iraq — and lifting Suez Canal flows by about 30%; roughly 62% of Yanbu&apos;s direct exports head to Asian buyers; after the July Houthi threat to Saudi port traffic put the Bab el-Mandeb exit at risk, Yanbu barrels increasingly route north into SUMED — 1.8 million b/d in late July, 46% of Yanbu exports — for Mediterranean loading and Cape of Good Hope voyages to Asia

Strategic role: The reason Saudi Arabia has options and Iraq has none — converts Gulf-basin production into Red Sea supply at the turn of a valve, feeding Yanbu&apos;s own refineries, direct Red Sea exports, and the SUMED land bridge beyond; in the 2026 crisis, the first link in the only chain (Petroline → Yanbu → SUMED → Sidi Kerir → Cape) that avoids both chokepoints entirely

Risks: Capacity ceiling — 5 million b/d against far larger Gulf-coast production means the line cannot carry the kingdom&apos;s full export program; midline vulnerability — pumping stations in open desert, already struck by drones in 2019; destination risk — Yanbu itself now sits inside the Houthi threat perimeter, which is what pushed flows onward into SUMED; and dependence on downstream links (SUMED capacity, Egyptian transit, Cape freight economics) that Aramco does not control</description>
			<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					38.24954936159315,23.97143160378243,0 38.50472567373777,24.15734388750042,0 49.66990672911108,25.92952433707718,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark id="062176C7573EB75DB130">
			<name>Druzhba pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline network — Russia → Central Europe; the world&apos;s largest oil pipeline system, the last Russian crude artery into the EU, and the clearest case on your map of a pipeline functioning as a geopolitical weapon in both directions

Route: Almetyevsk (Tatarstan) gathering Urals crude → Samara → Bryansk → Mozyr (Belarus), where it splits: the northern branch through Poland (Adamowo, Płock) to Germany&apos;s Schwedt and Leuna refineries; the southern branch through Ukraine (past the Brody hub) into Slovakia (Slovnaft, Bratislava) and Hungary (MOL&apos;s Duna refinery, Százhalombatta); the Czech spur off the southern leg fell silent in 2025 when TAL-PLUS made Prague independent. Total network roughly 5,500 km, in service since 1964 — &quot;Druzhba&quot; means &quot;friendship&quot;

Owner/Operator: No single owner — Transneft on the Russian trunk, with national operators segment by segment (Belarus, Ukraine&apos;s Ukrtransnafta, Poland&apos;s PERN); that fragmentation is exactly why every border on the route is a leverage point

Capacity: Northern branch designed for well over 1 million b/d, southern branch in the low hundreds of thousands b/d serving Hungary and Slovakia; the cargo has diverged politically — the north now moves Kazakh-branded barrels to Germany (Poland and Germany quit Russian crude voluntarily in January 2023), while the south still moves Russian Urals under the EU&apos;s pipeline exemption from the seaborne import ban

Operating history: A Cold War artery that outlived its era and became a bargaining chip: the 2019 organic-chloride contamination crisis poisoned the line for weeks; after 2022 it survived sanctions as the carve-out for landlocked members; through 2025, repeated Ukrainian strikes on its Russian infrastructure halted southbound flows three times, drawing formal complaints from Budapest and Bratislava to the European Commission

Recent data: The 2026 crisis was the system&apos;s worst: a drone strike on January 27 damaged infrastructure near the Brody hub, totally cutting Russian crude transit on the southern leg — most critical for Slovakia, which depends on the single Slovnaft refinery configured specifically for Russian crude, and which declared a national energy state of emergency. The three-month outage became a full diplomatic war: Hungary blocked the EU&apos;s 20th sanctions package and a €90 billion loan for Ukraine until oil deliveries resumed, Slovakia halted emergency electricity supplies to Ukraine, and both governments accused Kyiv of slow-walking repairs. Flows restarted on April 22 — unblocking the EU loan for Kyiv — with the political ground shifted by Orbán&apos;s election defeat on April 12. The lasting consequence: a permanent expansion of the Adria pipeline from Croatia, so Slovakia is never 100% dependent on Druzhba again

Strategic role: The inverse of every other asset on your map: elsewhere, infrastructure is attacked despite its cargo; here the cargo is the conflict — Russian oil revenue on one side, EU-member energy security on the other, and Ukraine holding the valve between them. Druzhba in 2026 proved a pipeline can be simultaneously a sanctions loophole, a war target, a veto generator inside the EU, and a hostage exchanged for a €90 billion loan

Risks: War damage from either side, with attribution itself contested; transit leverage stacked at every border (Ukraine over the south, Belarus over everything); policy extinction risk — the EU exemption is meant to be temporary, and each crisis strengthens the case for ending it; refinery lock-in, since Slovnaft and Duna are optimized for Urals and switching crude slates costs time and money; alternative-route inadequacy — the Adria line from the Croatian coast is the only substitute and needed emergency expansion; aging Soviet-era steel; and the 2019 precedent that quality, not just quantity, can be weaponized</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>29.25067063132974</longitude>
				<latitude>52.02993399437838</latitude>
