		<Placemark id="0A8D0C235F3EB7ABB22C">
			<name>Adria pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil import pipeline — Adriatic → Central and Southeastern Europe; the designated non-Russian supply route for the region&apos;s landlocked refineries, and the most fiercely disputed backup on your map

Route: Omišalj deep-water terminal on the island of Krk (Croatia) → Sisak, where the system splits: the northern branch runs via Virje (with a spur to Lendava, Slovenia) to Gola on the Hungarian border and on to MOL&apos;s Duna refinery at Százhalombatta — where it physically connects to the southern Druzhba system and onward to Slovnaft in Slovakia; the eastern branch runs from Sisak toward Slavonski Brod (spur into Bosnia) and the Serbian border, serving the NIS refineries; a short spur feeds Croatia&apos;s own Rijeka refinery

Owner/Operator: JANAF (Jadranski naftovod) — Croatian state-controlled; a Yugoslav-era project commissioned around 1980, built to import seaborne crude for the federation&apos;s refineries, disrupted by the 1990s wars, and reborn after 2022 as the region&apos;s strategic alternative to Russian pipeline supply

Capacity: The number at the heart of the fight: the Croatian side puts annual capacity toward Hungary at 11 to 15 million tonnes, but no more than about 2.2 million tonnes have ever actually been transported; sectional ratings tell the same story — roughly 10 million tonnes per year on the Hungarian leg and a maximum of 3.68 million tonnes on the Slovak section — while MOL&apos;s actual contract ran to only ~2.1 million tonnes

Operating history: Three lives: Yugoslav import artery, war casualty, and now geopolitical pivot. JANAF halted deliveries to Serbia&apos;s NIS in March 2022 as sanctions closed in, and since the EU&apos;s Russian-oil restrictions the line has been the theoretical answer to Hungarian and Slovak dependence — theoretical being the operative word, since proving sustained throughput is exactly what&apos;s now underway

Recent data: The 2026 Druzhba outage turned the dispute white-hot. With Russian flows cut for three months, MOL pressed JANAF to forward seaborne Russian crude, while Croatia assessed whether sanctions law even allowed it; MOL filed a formal complaint with the European Commission on March 4 accusing JANAF of abusing a dominant market position, alleging tariffs at multiples of the European average. The resolution mechanism: a roughly ten-month series of capacity tests that began March 11, run in phases with independent experts across seasons, weather and crude grades — reaching test rates of 40,000 tonnes per day by autumn, with clarity expected by early 2027. The deadline making it all urgent: Russian crude supplies are set to stop at the end of 2027, and the Druzhba crisis already produced a decision to permanently expand Adria&apos;s capacity so Slovakia is never fully dependent on Druzhba again

Strategic role: The same lesson as BTC, at the other end of Europe: an alternative that exists on the map but not yet in the physics — nominal capacity 15 million tonnes, demonstrated 2.2. And a second lesson layered on top: here the bottleneck isn&apos;t even steel — it&apos;s tariffs, contracts, sanctions interpretation and trust between two EU members. Adria proves that a route&apos;s true capacity is hydraulic and commercial and political, and it only counts when all three clear

Risks: Unproven sustained throughput until the tests conclude — with barely a year&apos;s margin before the 2027 cutoff; monopoly-pricing and access disputes now in Brussels&apos; hands; refinery lock-in on the demand side, since Duna and Slovnaft are configured for Urals and full conversion takes investment and time; single-terminal concentration at Omišalj; sanctions-interpretation risk that can freeze cargoes even when capacity exists; and the political triangle — Zagreb, Budapest, Bratislava — in which every technical question becomes a negotiation</description>
