		<Placemark id="0B66F491DC3F2390C62D">
			<name>Caspian Pipeline Consortium</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Caspian → Black Sea export system; landlocked Kazakhstan&apos;s main outlet and the only major oil pipeline crossing Russia not fully controlled by the Russian state

Route: Tengiz field (western Kazakhstan) → ~1,500 km across Kazakh and Russian territory via a chain of pumping stations → CPC Marine Terminal at Yuzhnaya Ozereevka near Novorossiysk (Russian Black Sea coast), loading tankers offshore through SPM buoys; carries the light sweet CPC Blend grade

Owner/Operator: Caspian Pipeline Consortium — a genuinely multinational shareholding: Transneft (Russia) as largest holder, KazMunayGas and Kazakhstan Pipeline Ventures, Chevron, Lukoil, Mobil Caspian (ExxonMobil), Rosneft-Shell Caspian Ventures, BG, Eni and Oryx; the consortium itself is not under Western sanctions

Capacity: ~1.5 million b/d; deliveries averaged 1.48 million b/d in 2026, with foreign shippers accounting for over 75% of the 70.5 million tonnes moved in 2025 — the barrels are mostly Kazakh (Chevron-led Tengizchevroil, Kashagan&apos;s NCOC), lifted by Western majors, exiting via a Russian port

Operating history: Commissioned in 2001 as the export solution for Tengiz; repeatedly disrupted since 2022 — storm damage to the mooring buoys, Russian regulatory suspension threats, a Ukrainian drone strike on the Kropotkinskaya pumping station in early 2025, and an attack on the SPM-2 loading buoy in November 2025 that previewed the current escalation

Recent data: Four tankers attacked by Ukrainian drones in four days in July 2026 while loading at the terminal — including vessels carrying Tengiz and Kashagan crude for Chevron- and Exxon-linked liftings, plus one sanctioned shadow-fleet tanker loading Rosneft Urals — forcing repeated suspensions; CPC Blend assessed at a $2.65/b discount to Dated Brent, with analysts seeing $1–2/b of differential upside and firmer Black Sea freight only if disruption stretches into weeks, heavy August West African and Latin American supply into the Mediterranean capping the move; traders&apos; core concern is behavioral — the terminal is undamaged, but vessels hesitate to berth under drones

Strategic role: The single artery carrying ~80% of the exports of Central Asia&apos;s largest producer — and the map&apos;s clearest case of collateral chokepoint risk: infrastructure targeted not for its own cargo but for its address, with Western-operated Kazakh barrels exposed to a war between two other states; the mirror of the Houthi playbook at Bab el-Mandeb — no blockade needed, one strike per few days keeps the route commercially unsafe

Risks: Drone and naval attack risk at the loading zone; war-risk insurance premiums and berthing refusals throttling liftings independently of physical damage; single-terminal concentration with no equivalent alternative — the fallback routes (Atyrau–Samara into the Russian system, Caspian barging toward Baku and the BTC line, the China pipeline) are fractions of CPC&apos;s capacity; Russian transit and regulatory leverage over the consortium; and differential risk for Kazakh producers whose netbacks price every one of these threats</description>
