		<Placemark id="09BE71D1E53F2D4290A0">
			<name>Khurmala-Fishkhabur pipeline (Kurdistan Regional Government&apos;s leg)</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — the only operating export path out of northern Iraq; built as an instrument of fiscal autonomy, now carrying the barrels of the government it was built to bypass

Route: Khurmala Dome near Erbil → Dohuk → Zakho → Fishkhabur; ~250 km, 40-inch trunk. Feeders from Taq Taq, Tawke/Peshkabir and Shaikan/Atrush; federal Kirkuk crude injects at Khurmala

Owner/Operator: KRG; built and operated by KAR Group

Capacity: ~700,000 b/d nameplate; actual throughput roughly a third of that

Operating history: Commissioned 2013–14, enabling independent KRG sales outside SOMO. Idled for export from March 2023 when the ICC award shut Ceyhan; restarted 27 September 2025; halted again in early 2026 over payments and authority; resumed 18 March 2026

Recent data: Over 4 million barrels shipped via Ceyhan in the months following the 2025 restart, with regional production averaging 200,000–205,000 b/d. The KRG delivers roughly 190,000 b/d to SOMO while retaining about 50,000 b/d for local consumption. Producers receive $14 per barrel after transport deductions, so contractor entitlement barrels are largely sold domestically at around $31 instead

Strategic role: The leverage asset. A regional government owns the only functioning link between federal fields and tidewater — which is why the budget dispute has a valve attached to it

Risks: Militia drone strikes on upstream facilities — Tawke installations were hit three times in 2025; payment mechanism instability driving producers to sell locally; single-operator concentration in KAR Group; and obsolescence risk once the federal line runs</description>
