		<Placemark id="03F676C2DF3F2D848ACF">
			<name>BTC pipeline (Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan)</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Caspian → Mediterranean corridor; the only large westbound export route that bypasses Russian territory entirely, and the designated fallback for Kazakh barrels when CPC is hit

Route: Sangachal terminal near Baku (Azerbaijan) → Georgia via Tbilisi → Ceyhan export terminal on Turkey&apos;s Mediterranean coast; ~1,770 km through eight pumping stations, crossing two mountain ranges; carries Azeri Light (BTC blend) from the ACG offshore complex plus transit volumes — Kazakh crude arrives by small tanker across the Caspian from Aktau to Baku before entering the line

Owner/Operator: BTC Co — operated by BP (~30%), with SOCAR (~25%), and minority stakes held by MOL, Equinor, TPAO, Eni, TotalEnergies, ITOCHU, INPEX, ExxonMobil and ONGC

Capacity: ~1.2 million b/d nameplate; structurally underutilized as Azerbaijan&apos;s ACG production declines — and that spare capacity is precisely what makes it available as the region&apos;s escape route

Operating history: Born of the 1994 &quot;Contract of the Century,&quot; commissioned in 2006 as the geopolitical project of its era — moving Caspian crude to market without touching Russia or adding tankers to the congested Bosphorus; stress-tested repeatedly: a 2008 explosion on the Turkish section during the Russia–Georgia war, and proximity to the 2020 Karabakh conflict, without long-term interruption

Recent data: The CPC drone campaign made BTC the active plan B — after the November 2025 strikes on CPC&apos;s moorings, Kazakhstan redirected some 300,000 tons of crude in December via BTC, the China line and Atyrau–Samara, and is targeting an increase in BTC-route exports in 2026, with a stated goal of 1.5–2.2 million tons for the year versus roughly 1.2 million tons in 2025. January–April 2026 flows ran 106,000–115,000 tons a month. Scale check: all of Kazakhstan&apos;s alternatives combined — BTC, Druzhba to Germany, the China pipeline — moved about 4.1 million tons, against roughly 60 million tons a year through CPC.

Strategic role: The insurance policy that can&apos;t cover the loss — politically the perfect route (no Russia, no Bosphorus, Mediterranean delivery), but physically capped not by the pipe, which has room, rather by the weakest link upstream: the fleet of small Caspian tankers shuttling Aktau to Baku. BTC proves a rule your whole map illustrates — a route&apos;s capacity is its narrowest segment, not its widest

Risks: The Caspian crossing as bottleneck — port capacity at Aktau and limited shuttle tonnage cap Kazakh transit at a fraction of CPC volumes; commercial dependence on Azerbaijan (SOCAR terms, tariff negotiations) for barrels that compete with Azeri crude; transit exposure across Georgia and eastern Turkey, with the 2008 sabotage as precedent; declining Azeri equity production, which frees capacity but weakens the line&apos;s own economics; and Ceyhan concentration risk — the terminal also serves the Iraq–Turkey line, stacking two countries&apos; contingency plans on one Mediterranean outlet</description>
