		<Placemark id="0C351F9C1A3F3A1EDA6E">
			<name>Southern Iraq Crude Oil Pipeline Network</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil gathering and export system — onshore trunk network + subsea loading lines; Iraq&apos;s primary (near-sole) export outlet

Route: Southern giant fields (Rumaila, West Qurna 1 &amp; 2, Zubair, Majnoon) → pumping stations and Zubair/Fao storage depots → Fao peninsula tank farm → subsea pipelines → Al-Basra Oil Terminal (ABOT) and SPM buoys in the Gulf; the Khor al-Amaya terminal sits largely idle alongside

Owner/Operator: Basra Oil Company (state, Ministry of Oil); exports marketed by state marketer SOMO; fields operated under technical service contracts with IOCs (BP at Rumaila, Lukoil at West Qurna 2, Eni at Zubair)

Capacity: Nominal export capacity ~3.4 million b/d; normal exports ~3.3 million b/d — about 93% of Iraq&apos;s crude exports — with oil funding roughly 90% of the state budget; grades: Basrah Medium, Basrah Heavy

Operating history: Built up from the 1950s–70s; terminals repeatedly bombed in the Iran–Iraq war and again in 1991, with Khor al-Amaya never fully restored; the 1975 north–south &quot;Strategic Pipeline&quot; linking Basra to the Kirkuk–Ceyhan corridor is long inoperative, and the IPSA line through Saudi Arabia was confiscated in 1990 and converted to gas — leaving the system with no functioning bypass to any other sea

Recent data: Seaborne exports collapsed to a dataset-low 260,000 b/d in May 2026 — 8% of the 2025 average — versus Saudi Arabia retaining 57% and the UAE 82% of export rates via pipeline reroutes; production halved to 1.9 million b/d in June from 4.2 million b/d in February; storage filled within days of the March closure, forcing shut-ins of southern fields, and tankers were struck in Iraqi waters near Basra; emergency workaround: 500–700 tanker trucks/day into Syria, feeding ~130,000 b/d of exports from Baniyas

Strategic role: Sole outlet for OPEC&apos;s second-largest producer, 100% Hormuz-dependent — the structural counter-example to Saudi Arabia&apos;s Yanbu/SUMED and the UAE&apos;s Fujairah bypass; in the 2026 crisis, the market&apos;s clearest proof that routes, not reserves, set supply

Risks: Hormuz transit as single point of failure; tanker strikes at the loading zone; storage saturation at Fao forcing field shut-ins; fiscal exposure (~90% of budget); no relief until a bypass is built — Basra–Haditha (2.5 million b/d, 700 km, toward Ceyhan and Baniyas) or the revived Basra–Aqaba line with Jordan (2.25 million b/d, ~$18 billion) — both years away</description>
