		<Placemark id="0F4DDE01573FA0A395D9">
			<name>Rotterdam - Rhine Pipeline (RRP)</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil and products pipeline system — North Sea → Rhine-Ruhr corridor; the Atlantic-gateway counterpart to TAL and SPSE, feeding Germany&apos;s industrial west from Europe&apos;s largest port

Route: Two systems totaling 457 km. Crude: a 36-inch, 177 km main line from Europoort via the Rozenburg pumping station to a tank terminal at Venlo (~210,000 m³ of storage), where it splits — north via a 43 km, 24-inch line toward Wesel and the Gelsenkirchen refinery in the Ruhr, and south via a 103 km, 24-inch line to Shell&apos;s Rheinland refinery near Cologne (Godorf and Wesseling plants), Germany&apos;s largest. Products: a separate 155 km line from Pernis to the German border near Venlo, moving petrol, naphtha, diesel, heating oil and jet fuel onward into the German product network

Owner/Operator: Rotterdam-Rijn Pijpleiding Maatschappij (RRP) — founded in 1958 as a joint venture of the majors; shareholders today are Shell, Ruhr Oel and bp. Intake comes from the big tank terminals at Botlek, Europoort and Maasvlakte plus the two Rotterdam refineries

Capacity: The system delivers around 25 million tonnes a year — roughly 16 million tonnes of crude and 9 million tonnes of products — entirely underground, running continuously since the early 1960s

Operating history: Built as the original artery of the Rotterdam model: crude lands at the deepest harbor in Europe and flows east to the refineries of the Rhine and Ruhr. Started as a 24-inch line, upgraded to 36-inch as demand grew — and now aging into a new role: its right-of-way is the backbone of the proposed Delta Corridor, a pipeline bundle along the existing route studied with the Port of Rotterdam for hydrogen toward the Chemelot cluster and North Rhine-Westphalia, with the long-term vision of hydrogen flowing east and captured CO2 returning west for North Sea storage

Recent data: RRP&apos;s world is being reshaped from both ends: downstream, its customer base is consolidating as German refining shrinks — Shell has been converting parts of the Rheinland complex away from crude processing — while upstream, Rotterdam&apos;s crude slate is the Atlantic Basin pool: North Sea, US and West African barrels that don&apos;t transit a single Middle East chokepoint. In the 2026 crisis that made the Rhine corridor structurally calmer on feedstock than the Med-fed south — while its product line runs hot, moving fuels inland from the ARA hub in a record-crack European diesel market

Strategic role: The third gateway on your map&apos;s European side — and the contrast that completes the picture: Bayernoil drinks from Trieste, Cressier from Fos, both priced off the disrupted Mediterranean; the Rhine-Ruhr refineries drink from Rotterdam, priced off the well-supplied Atlantic. Same continent, two crude pools, two very different 2026 stories — and VAROPreem&apos;s Rotterdam SAF plant is being built at the mouth of this system, where the port&apos;s logic is pivoting from moving crude east to moving new molecules both ways

Risks: Demand-side decline as its captive refineries convert or close — a pipeline&apos;s fate is its customers&apos;; concentration on two delivery points; energy-transition timing risk, with the Delta Corridor needing to mature before the crude business fades; port dependence on Rotterdam operations (congestion, strikes, North Sea weather); and the strategic footnote of 2026 — &quot;safe&quot; Atlantic supply still prices off a global market, so even Rotterdam barrels feel Hormuz in the flat price, just not in the freight</description>
