		<Placemark id="0732962C843FE62A3117">
			<name>SPSE (south european pipeline)</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline system — Mediterranean → inland Western Europe; the western twin of TAL, moving seaborne crude from the Rhône delta into France, Switzerland and historically Germany

Route: Fos-sur-Mer / Lavéra port complex (near Marseille) → Rhône and Saône valleys → delivery terminals at Lyon (St-Quentin-Fallavier, feeding the Feyzin refinery) and Besançon (Gennes) — where the crude passes into the Jura pipeline, owned by VARO, crossing into Switzerland to the Cressier refinery. The original main line ran 769 km to Karlsruhe; the network peaked at three lines totaling ~1,850 km

Owner/Operator: Société du Pipeline Sud-Européen — founded in 1958 by sixteen oil companies from the major world groups, still owned by its refiner-shippers; also a storage operator, running 40 floating-roof tanks totaling 2.26 million m³ — over 14 million barrels — at the Fos maritime terminal, holding around twenty crude grades in transit, with berths discharging tankers up to 400,000 tonnes at 15,000 m³/hour

Capacity: The living system today ships about 8 million tonnes of crude per year through five pumping stations to its two remaining customers; the terminal&apos;s scale — grade variety, VLCC-capable discharge, million-cubic-meter storage — is the real asset

Operating history: A monument to European refining&apos;s rise and retreat: first 34-inch line commissioned in December 1962 to feed the Rhine&apos;s refineries, two more lines added in 1971–72 at the industry&apos;s peak — then decades of refinery closures shrank the network. Today the 34-inch line operates only Fos-to-Besançon and is nitrogen-inerted beyond; the 40-inch Strasbourg line and 24-inch Lyon line are both inactive. Karlsruhe&apos;s MiRO now drinks exclusively from TAL

Recent data: The strategic pivot is the story: SPSE plans to convert its existing Lyon–Fos line to CO2 transport, with at least 4 million tonnes per year of capacity from 2030 — turning a declining crude artery into carbon-removal infrastructure for the industrial basin it once supplied. Meanwhile its remaining crude mission runs through the 2026 disruption like everything else on this map: the barrels discharged at Fos come from the same Mediterranean pool — Saharan Blend, Libyan, Azeri, CPC, Sidi Kerir loadings — that the crisis is repricing

Strategic role: For VAROPreem specifically, this is Cressier&apos;s lifeline — the only crude path to Switzerland&apos;s only refinery: tanker to Fos, SPSE to Gennes, VARO&apos;s own Jura line over the mountains. Put beside Bayernoil (tanker to Trieste, TAL over the Alps), a striking fact emerges: both of the group&apos;s landlocked refineries depend entirely on Mediterranean tanker arrivals, at opposite ends of the same sea

Risks: Structural decline — a two-customer system carries concentration risk in its purest form: if Feyzin ever stops refining crude, Cressier becomes the sole reason the line exists, and fixed costs land on fewer tonnes; single-corridor dependence for Cressier with no alternative supply route; Fos port exposure (strikes at the French Mediterranean terminals are a recurring feature, and NATO-region tensions now touch even domestic infrastructure); Mediterranean supply-pool risk in crisis years like this one; and transition-timing risk on the CO2 conversion — the new business must arrive before the old one fades</description>
