		<Placemark id="006A208CDA406775DAEF">
			<name>Sumed pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Red Sea → Mediterranean land bridge; Suez Canal complement and, in crisis, the only export path avoiding both Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb

Route: Ain Sokhna terminal (Gulf of Suez) → twin 42-inch lines, ~320 km overland across Egypt → Sidi Kerir terminal (Mediterranean, near Alexandria); Sidi Kerir holds ~19.5 million barrels of storage across 28 tanks and loads via SBM buoys serving tankers up to 500,000 DWT

Owner/Operator: Arab Petroleum Pipelines Company (SUMED) — EGPC (Egypt) 50%, Saudi Aramco 15%, UAE interests 15%, Kuwaiti interests 15%, Qatar 5%; a rare multi-Gulf-state joint venture on Egyptian soil

Capacity: ~2.5 million b/d combined; normal function is northbound Gulf-to-Europe flow paired with the Suez Canal — fully laden VLCCs cannot transit the canal, so they lighten at Ain Sokhna, pump the difference overland, and reload at Sidi Kerir

Operating history: Commissioned 1977 as the response to the canal&apos;s closure after the 1967 war and to the growth of supertankers too large for it; has served for decades as the structural link between Red Sea and Mediterranean crude markets, with utilization historically swinging with canal economics, freight rates and Gulf export patterns

Recent data: Flows surged after the March 2026 Hormuz closure pushed Saudi loadings to Yanbu via the 5 million b/d East-West pipeline, and the July Houthi threat to Saudi port traffic then endangered the Bab el-Mandeb exit; Yanbu crude bound for SUMED jumped to 1.8 million b/d in late July — up 66% week-on-week, nearly triple two weeks earlier, and 46% of Yanbu&apos;s exports — with Bab el-Mandeb transits falling ~30% in a single day; Asia-bound cargoes now load at Sidi Kerir and sail via the Cape of Good Hope

Strategic role: The escape valve of the 2026 crisis — the one route that turns Saudi barrels into Mediterranean barrels without touching either chokepoint; the structural counterpart to Iraq&apos;s bypass-less Basra system and the UAE&apos;s Fujairah line, and the reason Saudi Arabia retained 57% of exports while Iraq fell to 8%

Risks: Ceiling risk — utilization approaching the 2.5 million b/d capacity with limited headroom; upstream dependence on the Red Sea leg from Yanbu to Ain Sokhna, which is itself within Houthi reach; single-country transit exposure (Egypt) and multi-state ownership politics; demand-side risk that Asian buyers reject the longer, costlier Cape routing in favor of closer barrels; and reversion risk — if Hormuz normalizes, flows can collapse back toward canal-paired volumes as fast as they surged</description>
