		<Placemark>
			<name>TAL pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Adriatic → Central Europe trunk line; the single artery supplying the landlocked refining heart of the continent, and the line that ends, literally, at Bayernoil&apos;s gates

Route: SIOT Marine Terminal, Port of Trieste (Italy) → Friuli plain → over the Alps → Austria (with the AWP branch at Würmlach serving OMV&apos;s Schwechat refinery near Vienna) → Bavaria, where the system splits at the Ingolstadt/Vohburg hub: the western branch ends at Karlsruhe, the eastern branch at Neustadt — the two ends of VAROPreem&apos;s Bayernoil network — while at Vohburg the line connects to the Czech IKL pipeline feeding the tank farm at Nelahozeves and the Kralupy and Litvínov refineries; 753 km, 13 pumping stations

Owner/Operator: The TAL Group — three national companies (SIOT in Italy, TAL Österreich, Deutsche Transalpine Oelleitung) owned by a shipper consortium: OMV as largest holder with 32% and Shell with 19%, alongside Eni, ExxonMobil, Gunvor (C-Blue), Phillips 66, TotalEnergies, Czech MERO (5%), and Rosneft&apos;s ~11% German stake — held under German federal trusteeship since 2022

Capacity: ~43 million tonnes per year (roughly 850,000 b/d design); actual throughput runs near 37 million tonnes, with 2023 seeing 36.7 million tonnes discharged from 395 tankers at the Marine Terminal, and cumulative volumes past 1.7 billion tonnes; the system covers 100% of southern Germany&apos;s crude needs, 90% of Austria&apos;s, and — since the expansion — the whole of Czechia&apos;s

Operating history: A Cold War-era engineering feat — built in 1,000 days from December 1964, financed by a syndicate of 83 banks, commissioned in 1967 — that has quietly become more strategic with age: first as Bavaria and Austria&apos;s lifeline, then, after 2022, as the instrument of Czech independence from Russian oil. The TAL-PLUS project raised deliveries to Czechia by 4 million tonnes per year, bringing the IKL&apos;s supply to 8 million tonnes annually — achieved with no new pipe, just upgraded pumps and motors for around €44 million — completed in April 2025, with North Sea crude delivered to Trieste and pumped north as Druzhba imports ended

Recent data: In the 2026 crisis, TAL is the demand side of the Mediterranean story your map tells: every barrel it moves arrives by tanker at Trieste from the same repriced pool — Azeri, Saharan, Libyan, CPC reroutes, Sidi Kerir loadings, Atlantic Basin cargoes — meaning the feedstock costs of eight inland refineries in three countries, Bayernoil included, are set by Mediterranean freight, differentials and war-risk premiums decided a thousand kilometers south

Strategic role: For VAROPreem, this is the umbilical: both Bayernoil sites sit at the eastern terminus, so the group&apos;s largest conventional asset runs entirely on TAL barrels — the mirror of Cressier on SPSE, one refinery per Mediterranean gateway; for Europe, it&apos;s the continent&apos;s quiet single point of dependence — the only crude source for southern Germany, nearly all of Austria, and now all of Czechia, which traded Druzhba risk for Trieste concentration

Risks: Concentration by design — 100% supply shares mean a prolonged outage has no substitute for three countries&apos; refining; Trieste as maritime single point of entry (port strikes, Adriatic weather, and the Med&apos;s 2026 security environment); allocation tension as Czech volumes added since 2025 push utilization toward the ceiling, putting eight refineries in competition for space; Alpine transit with high environmental stakes; and governance friction baked into the shareholder table, where a trusteed Russian stake sits alongside the states that engineered independence from Russian oil</description>
