		<Placemark>
			<name>Abu Dhabi Crude Oil Pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil pipeline — Gulf → Gulf of Oman trans-UAE bypass; the only purpose-built export line whose sole function is to avoid the Strait of Hormuz entirely

Route: Habshan processing hub (Abu Dhabi onshore, gathering the Murban system) → ~380 km eastward across the emirates and the Hajar mountains → Fujairah export terminal on the Gulf of Oman, seaward of the Strait; the terminal sits inside one of the world&apos;s largest bunkering and storage hubs, including underground rock caverns

Owner/Operator: ADNOC — Abu Dhabi state oil company; the line was developed with strategic intent by the government and folded into ADNOC&apos;s export infrastructure

Capacity: ~1.5 million b/d nameplate; loads principally the Murban grade — Abu Dhabi&apos;s flagship crude and the underlying of the IFAD Murban futures contract, making Fujairah both a physical outlet and a pricing point beyond the chokepoint

Operating history: Commissioned in 2012, explicitly as chokepoint insurance after years of Iranian threats to close Hormuz; the logic was validated early — the 2019 tanker sabotage incidents occurred off Fujairah itself — but like Petroline, the line ran for over a decade as a hedge before 2026 turned it into the UAE&apos;s primary export artery

Recent data: The pipeline is why the UAE outperformed every Gulf producer in the crisis — the country maintained 82% of its 2025 export rate in May, versus 57% for Saudi Arabia and 8% for Iraq, rerouting volumes via pipeline and adding a shuttle service moving barrels to ship-to-ship transfers offshore Oman; the UAE now plans to double its export capacity outside Hormuz with a second pipeline to Fujairah

Strategic role: The cleanest solution to the chokepoint problem on the map — where Saudi barrels need a three-link chain (Petroline → Yanbu → SUMED) to escape both straits, Emirati barrels need one pipe; Fujairah&apos;s storage, bunkering and futures-delivery infrastructure compounds the advantage, letting Abu Dhabi not just export through the crisis but trade through it

Risks: Capacity ceiling — 1.5 million b/d against much larger UAE production means everything above the pipe still needs Hormuz; single-corridor concentration — one line, pumping stations and a single terminal zone form an obvious target set, and Fujairah has been attacked before (2019); congestion risk as the whole region&apos;s diverted volumes, STS operations and bunker demand converge on one small stretch of coast; and the standing reality that the Gulf of Oman is beyond the Strait but not beyond Iranian reach</description>
			<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					53.61652943762361,23.82677165684339,0 55.73844495787689,24.23826558587521,0 56.35414850314482,25.1987654300518,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Trans-Sakhalin Oil Pipeline — Sakhalin-2</name>
			<description>Sakhalin - 2 route

What is the Sakhalin-2 project?

Sakhalin Energy operates the Sakhalin-2 Project.

It is one of Russia’s largest integrated:

offshore oil,
offshore gas,
LNG export projects.
The project includes:

1. Offshore fields

Main offshore fields:

Piltun-Astokhskoye
Lunskoye
These fields produce:

crude oil,
natural gas.

2. Pipelines across Sakhalin Island

Hydrocarbons are transported southward across the island through pipelines.


3. Prigorodnoye LNG plant

At the south of the island:

gas is liquefied into LNG,
oil is exported by tanker.
This is where the cargo in your article was loaded.


What is Sakhalin Blend crude?

“Sakhalin Blend” is the crude oil exported from the Sakhalin-2 project.

It is:

light crude,
sweet crude (low sulfur),
attractive for Asian refiners.
The interesting point is that this oil is partly produced alongside LNG operations.

That is why sanctions exemptions exist:

if Japan stopped taking Sakhalin crude, it could disrupt the entire LNG chain feeding Japan.


Why is Japan still importing it?

Japan depends heavily on LNG imports for power generation.

The Sakhalin-2 Project supplies LNG directly to Japanese utilities because:

shipping distance is short,
supply is stable,
Japan already owns minority stakes through:
Mitsui &amp; Co.
Mitsubishi Corporation
So the US and G7 created a specific exemption allowing Japan to continue importing Sakhalin-related crude and LNG.</description>
