		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>OK1 pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil trunk pipeline — Sahara → Mediterranean; one of the north-flowing arteries of Algeria&apos;s export system, feeding the country&apos;s largest refinery and the Skikda loading terminal

Route: Haoud El Hamra dispatching hub (25 km northeast of Hassi Messaoud, where Algeria&apos;s Saharan crude production converges) → ~645 km north across the desert and the Atlas ranges via a chain of pumping stations → Skikda industrial and port complex on the Mediterranean coast; a 34-inch line in service since 1972. The Sonatrach naming code reads route-by-letter: K for Skikda, Z for Arzew, B for Bejaïa — OK1 is the Skikda crude line, alongside sisters OZ1 (Arzew, the country&apos;s first, 805 km) and OB1 (Bejaïa)

Owner/Operator: Sonatrach — Algerian state hydrocarbons company, via its Transport par Canalisation (TRC) division; at Haoud El Hamra the OK1 system runs eight 51,200 m³ floating-roof crude tanks plus condensate tankage, and a parallel condensate line (NK1) also serves Skikda

Capacity: Sized to feed the Skikda refinery — Algeria&apos;s largest — plus crude export loadings at the terminal; the line has undergone pumping-station extension and renovation works specifically to raise transport capacity, part of a broader rehabilitation of Sonatrach&apos;s aging northern network

Operating history: Commissioned in 1972 as part of the buildout that made Skikda Algeria&apos;s eastern energy gateway — refinery, LNG plant and export port in one complex; the line has run for five decades through the same challenges as the rest of the network: desert conditions, aging steel, and the security legacy of the 1990s–2010s, managed through successive ENAC/Sarpi rehabilitation campaigns rather than replacement

Recent data: The crude it carries is Saharan Blend — light, sweet, very low sulfur, among the highest-quality export grades in the Mediterranean basin. In the 2026 market that matters twice over: Saharan Blend sits in exactly the Med light sweet pool — alongside Libya&apos;s Es Sider, rerouted CPC Blend and Azeri barrels — that European refiners lean on while Gulf supply is constrained by Hormuz, and the Skikda refinery sells products into the same record European cracks; short pipeline distance, no chokepoint transit, and a two-day sail to European ports make Algerian barrels among the most logistics-advantaged crude in the basin right now

Strategic role: The quiet winner on the map — Algeria&apos;s export system touches no strait, no canal, no war zone: field to Mediterranean water entirely within one country, days from the customer; in a year when the market prices routes over reserves, OK1 is what a de-risked route looks like, and the counterweight to every stressed asset on this project

Risks: Infrastructure age — the rehabilitation programs exist because the steel demands them, and sister line OB1 needed 164 km rebuilt to recover its design capacity; concentration at Haoud El Hamra, the single hub through which the country&apos;s crude dispatching flows; upstream dependence on the mature Hassi Messaoud basin, where decline rates rather than transit risk cap volumes; desert transit security as a managed but permanent condition; and allocation tension between feeding the domestic refinery and maximizing export barrels when Mediterranean prices reward the latter</description>
