		<Placemark>
			<name>Gialo-Waha pipeline</name>
			<styleUrl>#m_ylw-pushpin</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					21.41375616812475,28.6894359166971,0 19.92278524568038,28.29901033529408,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Samah/Dahra-Es Sider pipeline</name>
			<description>Classification: Crude oil trunk pipeline — Sirte Basin gathering line; the artery connecting the Waha field cluster to Libya&apos;s largest export terminal, and a chronic single point of failure in the country&apos;s supply

Route: Samah field (Al Wahat district) and Dahra field (Sirte district), deep in the Sirte Basin desert → 32-inch line running north → Es Sider terminal on the Gulf of Sirte, Libya&apos;s biggest oil port with ~350,000 b/d of export capacity; the crude loads as Es Sider, the country&apos;s flagship grade

Owner/Operator: Waha Oil Company — Libyan NOC 59.2%, ConocoPhillips 16.3%, TotalEnergies 16.3% (acquired from Marathon in 2018), Hess 8.2%; one of the last places US majors still lift equity crude in Libya

Capacity: The Waha fields can pump around 300,000 b/d, with production recently cited near 285,000 b/d; pipeline shutdowns have historically cut national output by up to 200,000 b/d at a stroke — the line, not the fields, sets the ceiling

Operating history: A study in infrastructure decay: NOC has described the line as dilapidated, plagued by leaks and starved of maintenance budget through a decade of civil war, militia attacks and terminal battles — Es Sider&apos;s tank farm burned in the 2014–16 &quot;Oil Crescent&quot; fighting, and the system has cycled through blockades (2020, 2022), Petroleum Facilities Guard strikes, and emergency repair shutdowns in 2021 and 2022, each time knocking out roughly 200,000 b/d

Recent data: Libya&apos;s relevance in 2026 runs through the Mediterranean light sweet market: Es Sider&apos;s high middle-distillate yield makes it a favorite of Mediterranean and Northwest European refiners — exactly the barrel pool competing with rerouted CPC Blend and the August wave of West African and Latin American supply that analysts cite as the cap on Black Sea differentials; with Gulf supply constrained by Hormuz, every reliable Libyan cargo is worth more, and every outage on this line tightens the same pool

Strategic role: The map&apos;s reminder that chokepoints aren&apos;t only maritime — this is an onshore chokepoint, where one corroded 32-inch pipe in the desert moves a fifth of a country&apos;s production; in a year when the market prices routes over reserves, Libya&apos;s risk sits not in a strait but in its own unmaintained steel

Risks: Mechanical failure as the base case — leak-driven shutdowns are routine, not exceptional; militia and political risk at both ends (field blockades upstream, PFG action and NOC–Central Bank revenue feuds at the terminal); force majeure frequency that makes Es Sider supply structurally unreliable for term buyers; underinvestment locked in by Libya&apos;s fiscal chaos; and concentration risk — the same Gulf of Sirte cluster (Es Sider, Ras Lanuf) can be blockaded as a unit, as history has repeatedly shown</description>
