		<Placemark>
			<name>RMR</name>
			<description>Classification: Refined-products pipeline — the Rhine-Main-Rohrleitung (RMR), Germany&apos;s main north–south products artery
Route: Dutch border at Venlo → Herongen → Dinslaken → Ratingen → Cologne-Godorf / Wesseling → through the Eifel and Hunsrück (Ramersbach, Schwall) → fork at the Udenheim pump station near Mainz → (a) Gustavsburg / Flörsheim / Raunheim depots for the Frankfurt area and airport, (b) Ludwigshafen / BASF Oppau; 525 km, fully underground, nine pump stations (32,000 kW)
Owner/Operator: Rhein-Main-Rohrleitungstransportgesellschaft mbH (RMR), Cologne-Godorf — founded 1965 by Deutsche Shell, joined by BP and Esso; shareholders Shell, BP, Esso/ExxonMobil
Capacity: ~15 million m³ (~12 million tonnes) of products a year — equivalent to some 400,000 road tankers
Handles: Batches of diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet fuel and petrochemical raw materials, dispatched in planned sequence
Connectivity: Fed at Venlo by the products line of the Rotterdam–Rhine Pipeline (RRP) from the Rotterdam refineries, then topped up at Cologne with output from the Godorf/Wesseling refineries; connected tank farms at Homberg, Dinslaken, Cologne-Niehl, Gustavsburg, Flörsheim, Raunheim and Ludwigshafen; connected plants and refineries at Bottrop, Dormagen, Godorf, Wesseling and Oppau
Recent data: A €100m+ modernisation programme with a new control centre opened at Godorf in 2025; the operator stresses that as German crude processing declines — most recently the shutdown of Shell&apos;s Wesseling distillation unit — the pipeline&apos;s role in supply security is increasing; the line ends at Frankfurt/Ludwigshafen — it does NOT extend to Karlsruhe, Strasbourg or Basel
Strategic role: The counter-example to the Rhine chokepoint — regions it serves (Frankfurt Airport, BASF Ludwigshafen) held up in the 2018, 2022 and 2026 low-water crises, while barge-only markets further upstream (Strasbourg, Basel) faced shortages and record freight; the physical boundary between &quot;pipeline-protected&quot; and &quot;Kaub-exposed&quot; southern Germany runs at Ludwigshafen
Risks: Single-system dependence for Rhine-Main jet fuel; incident history (1991 rupture near Dinslaken); shareholder-driven priorities; rising strategic weight without a physical extension south</description>
			<styleUrl>#msn_icon?color=1976d2&amp;id=2000&amp;scale=4</styleUrl>
