		<Placemark>
			<name>Saharan Blend route</name>
			<description>API: ~45° (light)
Sulfur: ~0.1% (very low)
Type: Light, sweet crude — a commingled export blend, not a single field.
Origin: Algeria; blended from Saharan fields (dominated by Hassi Messaoud, gathered at Haoud El Hamra) and exported through Skikda, Arzew or Bejaia terminals.
Markets: Mainly the Mediterranean and Northwest Europe, with additional sales to the Americas.
Used for: High light-distillate yield — naphtha, gasoline, jet fuel and diesel — plus petrochemical feed; its low sulphur means minimal desulphurisation cost, ideal for simpler and mid-complexity refineries.
Pricing: Monthly Sonatrach OSP set as a variable differential to Dated Brent, frequently at a premium.
Vessels: Usually Aframax; Suezmax for larger cargoes.
Transit: Around 2 days / ~450 nm from Skikda to Fos-sur-Mer across the western Mediterranean; ~5–6 days to Rotterdam via the Strait of Gibraltar.
Risks: Low — no major chokepoints; mainly Gulf of Lion mistral weather, port congestion and occasional Algerian loading or pipeline disruptions (Gibraltar transit only if routed to the Atlantic).</description>
			<styleUrl>#stylemap_id020</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					6.939508765107738,36.88880302234791,0 6.597759024239593,37.60688030029786,0 5.79435056388273,38.80280264287649,0 5.192549632927312,40.50242277568254,0 4.938327436476548,43.38064883167617,0 4.901126363949658,43.41450603818715,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Es Sider route</name>
			<description>API: ~37° (light)
Sulfur: ~0.4% (sweet)
Type: Light, sweet crude — a commercial export blend named after its terminal, fed by several Sirte Basin fields.
Origin: Libya; gathered from Waha, Gialo and other Sirte Basin fields and exported through the Es Sider terminal via offshore Single Buoy Moorings (SBMs).
Markets: Mainly the Mediterranean and Northwest Europe (Italy, Spain, France, Germany, the Netherlands), with occasional sales to Asia.
Used for: High gasoline and middle-distillate yield with low sulphur; well suited to Mediterranean and mid-complexity refineries.
Pricing: Monthly NOC/Waha OSP set as a variable differential to Dated Brent, with a Libyan political-risk element.
Vessels: Usually Suezmax or Aframax, loaded offshore at the SBMs.
Transit: Around 5–6 days / ~1,300–1,450 nm from Es Sider to Fos-sur-Mer via the Strait of Sicily; ~7–9 days to Rotterdam via the Strait of Gibraltar.
Risks: Libyan political instability — field shutdowns, port blockades and force majeure at the Es Sider terminal, plus militia/security risk; Strait of Sicily traffic density en route.</description>
			<styleUrl>#stylemap_id25</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					18.35596912396328,30.68834646924192,0 13.54114224798351,35.22509704572018,0 13.3339340684015,36.43726026793413,0 8.117250326889776,38.11726405364553,0 4.935933745798977,43.42443173647109,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Girassol route</name>
			<description>API: ~30° (medium)
Sulfur: ~0.34% (sweet)
Type: Medium, sweet crude — the commingled Girassol + Jasmim + Rosa stream processed on the Girassol FPSO.
Origin: Angola; produced in deepwater Block 17 and exported directly offshore from the FPSO via a CALM buoy — no onshore terminal or pipeline.
Markets: Mainly China (over half of Angolan exports) and India, with a material share to Europe (led by Spain and the Netherlands).
Used for: Good gasoil and middle-distillate yield with low sulphur; suits complex Northwest European and Asian cracking refineries.
Pricing: Variable differential to Dated Brent, in line with West African official selling prices.
Vessels: Suezmax to Europe; VLCC for the long-haul Asian trade — vessel size affects the discharge port, as a laden VLCC is draft-limited to the deepest terminals.
Transit: Around 20–24 days / ~6,400–6,900 nm from Block 17 up the Atlantic around West Africa to Lysekil; longer to Asia via the Cape of Good Hope.
Risks: Gulf of Guinea piracy and armed-robbery risk at the loading end; long Atlantic and North Sea weather exposure; VLCC draft limits at discharge. No Suez, Hormuz or Gibraltar transit.</description>
			<styleUrl>#stylemap_id240</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					11.68003301331978,-7.649577622331934,0 -1.873013776979757,0.04630934251578204,0 -12.06806614713823,2.294516650441873,0 -17.9530775774571,6.433334333849768,0 -19.41022956915689,9.028844896546691,0 -20.10758865907178,9.701624060040059,0 -20.95851789014388,15.18023622844132,0 -21.910099231122,23.14525290950652,0 -19.37403363905658,32.64498855561517,0 -17.68375646879597,38.1182129512826,0 -14.5561826832905,41.62259646124227,0 -6.93492744323768,48.00313721776582,0 -4.864273874044693,49.49321026658558,0 0.9815128998333167,50.72562941224943,0 4.124782963109086,51.9479733615237,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Dalia crude route</name>
			<description>Crude: Dalia
API: 23.3–23.7°
Sulfur: 0.49–0.51%
Type: Heavy, sweet offshore crude
Origin: Dalia Field, Block 17, offshore Angola
Markets: Mainly China; also Spain, France and the Netherlands
Used for: Low-sulfur fuel oil, middle distillates and heavy refinery products
Pricing: Traded at a variable differential to Dated Brent; premium or discount depends on Asian demand, freight and refinery margins
Vessels: Loaded offshore from the Dalia FPSO, typically on Suezmax or VLCC tankers; Suezmax is suitable for Rotterdam
Transit: Approximately 17–21 days to Rotterdam
Risks: Offshore-loading delays, Atlantic weather, piracy risk in the Gulf of Guinea, and congestion in the English Channel and Dover Strait</description>
			<styleUrl>#stylemap_id220</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					11.76544131761719,-7.681535226026257,0 4.536441809271747,-2.835720222568552,0 -1.614842598021807,0.2763960812980644,0 -12.00129151352508,2.551203955325124,0 -17.70786186250704,6.581071791601223,0 -19.82995782259071,9.839589195019903,0 -21.61931288423892,23.43855453158856,0 -19.53481159370532,30.67855054242659,0 -17.52297741361756,38.04926625872795,0 -4.761200990858232,49.43662018052165,0 0.9701809793403537,50.74199319155846,0 4.131082550290229,51.9482318168594,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Liza route</name>
			<description>API: ~32° (medium)
Sulfur: ~0.59% (sweet)
Type: Medium, sweet crude — a single-development stream from the Liza Destiny FPSO (distinct from Unity Gold and Payara Gold).
Origin: Guyana; produced offshore in the Stabroek Block and loaded directly from the FPSO by ship-to-ship transfer — no onshore terminal or pipeline.
Markets: Predominantly Europe (about two-thirds of Guyanese exports in 2024), with additional sales to the United States and Asia.
Used for: Strong middle-distillate, diesel and jet yield; low sulphur makes it a favoured IMO-compliant feed for complex European refineries.
Pricing: Variable differential to Dated Brent (the &quot;Latin Dated Brent&quot; strip), recently around +$1.30/bbl.
Vessels: Usually Suezmax; some Aframax, loaded offshore via tandem transfer.
Transit: Around 13–16 days / ~4,400–4,800 nm transatlantic from the Liza field to Lysekil via the English Channel (or north-about Scotland), North Sea and Skagerrak.
Risks: North Atlantic and North Sea weather, Dover Strait/Channel traffic density and the Skagerrak approach; offshore-loading weather windows. No piracy or chokepoint exposure on the route.</description>
			<styleUrl>#stylemap_id200</styleUrl>
			<LineString>
				<tessellate>1</tessellate>
				<coordinates>
					-56.9169407616409,8.017149440466609,0 -53.98962135433751,16.20685518031413,0 -35.64261635163013,39.94978219636562,0 -4.948882600512566,49.5367256147621,0 0.9926929166654497,50.74272144205962,0 4.131152803618104,51.94452678257312,0 
				</coordinates>
			</LineString>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Bonny light route</name>
			<description>Crude: Bonny Light
API: ~32.9°
Sulfur: ~0.16%
Type: Light, very sweet Nigerian crude
Origin: Niger Delta; exported FOB from the Bonny Terminal, Nigeria
Markets: Mainly Europe and Asia, including the Netherlands, France, India and China
Used for: Gasoline, diesel, gasoil, jet fuel and vacuum gasoil
Pricing: Usually priced at a premium or discount to Dated Brent; during the March–April 2026 supply crisis, Bonny Light reached premiums of roughly +$7.50 to above +$10/bbl
Vessels: Typically loaded in standard cargoes of about 950,000 barrels, generally on Suezmax tankers
Transit: Around 14–18 days from Bonny Terminal to Rotterdam
Risks: Pipeline theft and sabotage, loading disruptions, Niger Delta security, Gulf of Guinea piracy, Atlantic weather and congestion in the English Channel</description>
