	<Folder>
		<name>Inland Waterways</name>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Kaub</name>
			<description>Site: Kaub gauge — reference water level for Rhine navigation
Classification: Logistics chokepoint — the shallowest stretch of the Middle Rhine, between Mainz and Koblenz
Location: Kaub, Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany
Operated by: German Federal Waterways and Shipping Administration (WSV)
Role: The single reading the fuel and freight markets watch to decide barge loadings between ARA and southern Germany / Switzerland — normal August level ~200 cm; below the 78 cm equivalent water level most barges must lighten cargo; around 40 cm shipping runs in emergency mode
Recent data: All-time record low in August 2026 — ~21 cm on 3–4 August, ~16–17 cm on 10–11 August, below 10 cm on 14 August; forecast to hover near 20 cm before edging above 30 cm around 23 August
Strategic role: A textbook case of a physical constraint setting prices — the gauge is where a weather event becomes a freight premium and an inland supply squeeze
Risks: Third major low-water shock since 2018; heat waves and low soil moisture across southern Germany; recovery dependent on weeks of sustained rain</description>
			<LookAt>
				<longitude>7.762195600000029</longitude>
				<latitude>50.0880599</latitude>
				<altitude>0</altitude>
				<heading>-9.621177911427134e-14</heading>
				<tilt>0</tilt>
				<range>9609.166468307194</range>
				<gx:altitudeMode>relativeToSeaFloor</gx:altitudeMode>
			</LookAt>
			<styleUrl>#msn_firedept</styleUrl>
			<Point>
				<gx:drawOrder>1</gx:drawOrder>
				<coordinates>7.762195600000029,50.0880599,0</coordinates>
			</Point>
		</Placemark>
		<Placemark>
			<name>Rhin</name>
			<description>Classification: Inland waterway — the barge corridor linking the ARA hub to southern Germany, Alsace and Switzerland
Route: Rotterdam (Europoort) → Duisburg → Cologne → Koblenz → Kaub → Mainz → Ludwigshafen/Mannheim → Karlsruhe → Strasbourg → Basel; ~800 km of navigable Rhine
Handles: Diesel, gasoline, heating oil, jet fuel and chemical feedstocks moving inland from ARA refineries and storage; coal, grains, ores and containers
Connectivity: Feeds inland fuel depots and refineries — including Strasbourg&apos;s Rhine port depots and the Basel gateway that supplies Switzerland and VAROPreem&apos;s Cressier refinery
Status: Record low water in August 2026 — the Kaub gauge fell to ~21 cm on 3–4 August (lowest since records began in 1880, below the 25 cm record of October 2018) and under 10 cm around 14 August; a rebound above ~30 cm was not expected before late August, and no return to normal before October
Recent data: Barges limited to roughly a third of normal cargo (~460 t through Kaub instead of ~1,200 t); Rotterdam–south Germany product freight up ~400% in two months; shippers switching to rail and road — about 60 trucks to replace one 1,500 t barge
Strategic role: The physical link between the ARA pricing hub and inland demand — when the river drops, freight surges and inland product prices decouple from Rotterdam (in 2018 the Rotterdam–Basel fuel transport cost went from ~$5/bbl to over $35/bbl)
Risks: Recurring summer droughts (2018, 2022, 2026); Kaub as a single shallow chokepoint; limited pipeline and rail substitution capacity for Switzerland and Alsace (Strasbourg city)</description>
